window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2005-142", year: 2005, no: 142, subject: "modern", topic: "Karachi resolution 1931",
  q: `Who among the following drafted the resolution on fundamental rights for the Karachi Session of Congress in 1931?`,
  opts: ["Dr. B. R. Ambedkar", "Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru", "Dr. Rajendra Prasad", "Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru</h3>
<p>The <b>Karachi session</b> (March 1931, presided over by <b>Vallabhbhai Patel</b>) adopted the Resolution on <b>Fundamental Rights and Economic Policy</b>, drafted by <b>Jawaharlal Nehru</b> (with M.N. Roy's influence). It promised civil liberties, universal adult franchise, equality, a living wage, free primary education, and state control of key industries.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Ambedkar later chaired the Constitution's Drafting Committee; he did not draft the Karachi resolution.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Rajendra Prasad did not draft it.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Patel presided over the session; he did not draft the resolution.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Karachi also endorsed the Gandhi–Irwin Pact and mourned Bhagat Singh, Rajguru and Sukhdev (hanged 23 March 1931).</li>
<li>Earlier rights demands: Constitution of India Bill (1895, Tilak), Nehru Report (1928) with 19 fundamental rights.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2005-143", year: 2005, no: 143, subject: "modern", topic: "UP Kisan Sabha 1918",
  q: `Who among the following was not associated with the formation of U.P. Kisan Sabha in February 1918?`,
  opts: ["Indra Narain Dwivedi", "Gauri Shankar Misra", "Jawaharlal Nehru", "Madan Mohan Malaviya"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Jawaharlal Nehru</h3>
<p>The <b>UP Kisan Sabha</b> was founded in February 1918 by <b>Gauri Shankar Misra and Indra Narain Dwivedi</b>, with the support of <b>Madan Mohan Malaviya</b>, through the Home Rule League's efforts. <b>Nehru</b> got involved with the Awadh peasant movement only in <b>1920</b> (Pratapgarh, with Baba Ramchandra).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Indra Narain Dwivedi was a founder.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Gauri Shankar Misra was a founder.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Malaviya supported its formation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Awadh Kisan Sabha (1920, Baba Ramchandra, Nehru) split from the UP Kisan Sabha over Non-Cooperation; Eka movement (1921–22, Madari Pasi).</li>
<li>All India Kisan Sabha (1936, Lucknow; Swami Sahajanand Saraswati president, N.G. Ranga secretary).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2005-144", year: 2005, no: 144, subject: "modern", topic: "Gandhi's early satyagrahas",
  q: `Which pairs are correctly matched?
<table><tr><th></th><th>Movement/Satyagraha</th><th>Person actively associated with</th></tr>
<tr><td>1.</td><td>Champaran</td><td>Rajendra Prasad</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.</td><td>Ahmedabad Mill Workers</td><td>Morarji Desai</td></tr>
<tr><td>3.</td><td>Kheda</td><td>Vallabhbhai Patel</td></tr></table>
Select the correct answer:`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "2 and 3", "1 and 3", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3</h3>
<h3>Pair by pair</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pair 1 (correct):</b> Rajendra Prasad (then a lawyer) worked with Gandhi in Champaran (1917).</li>
<li><b>Pair 2 (wrong):</b> the Ahmedabad mill strike (1918) involved <b>Anasuya Sarabhai</b> (who led the workers; her brother Ambalal Sarabhai led the mill owners). Morarji Desai was not involved.</li>
<li><b>Pair 3 (correct):</b> <b>Vallabhbhai Patel</b> joined Gandhi in the Kheda satyagraha (1918) and left his legal practice.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Gandhi's first hunger strike was during the Ahmedabad strike (35% wage hike awarded). The Ahmedabad Textile Labour Association (Majoor Mahajan Sangh, 1920) followed.</li>
<li>Patel earned the title "Sardar" in Bardoli (1928).</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The source copy labels the pairs A, B, C while the codes use 1, 2, 3; they are shown here as 1, 2, 3.</div>` },

{ id: "2005-145", year: 2005, no: 145, subject: "modern", topic: "Kunwar Singh",
  q: `Which one of the following places did Kunwar Singh, a prominent leader of the Revolt of 1857, belong to?`,
  opts: ["Bihar", "Madhya Pradesh", "Rajasthan", "Uttar Pradesh"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Bihar</h3>
<p><b>Kunwar Singh</b> (about 1777–1858), the zamindar of <b>Jagdishpur</b> (Bhojpur, near Arrah, Bihar), led the 1857 revolt in Bihar at about 80. He fought guerrilla campaigns across UP and central India, and died of wounds on 26 April 1858, days after defeating the British near Jagdishpur.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Madhya Pradesh leaders: Rani Lakshmibai fought at Gwalior; Rani Avantibai (Ramgarh).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Rajasthan: Kotah revolt; Thakur Kushal Singh of Auwa.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Uttar Pradesh leaders: Nana Sahib (Kanpur), Begum Hazrat Mahal (Lucknow), Khan Bahadur Khan (Bareilly).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>23 April is celebrated in Bihar as "Vijayotsav" for Kunwar Singh. His brother Amar Singh continued the fight.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2005-146", year: 2005, no: 146, subject: "modern", topic: "1857: areas affected",
  q: `Which one of the following territories was not affected by the Revolt of 1857?`,
  opts: ["Jhansi", "Chittor", "Jagdishpur", "Lucknow"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Chittor</h3>
<p><b>Chittor</b> (Rajasthan, Mewar) saw no significant revolt in 1857; Rajputana's princes largely stayed loyal to the British, though there were risings at Kotah, Nasirabad, Neemuch and Auwa.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Jhansi: Rani Lakshmibai.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Jagdishpur (Bihar): Kunwar Singh.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Lucknow: Begum Hazrat Mahal; siege of the Residency.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Areas largely unaffected: Punjab, Bengal (except Barrackpore), most of the south (Madras, Bombay presidencies), Rajputana, Hyderabad.</li>
<li>Chittorgarh fort is part of the "Hill Forts of Rajasthan" World Heritage Site (2013); famous for the jauhar of Rani Padmini.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2005-147", year: 2005, no: 147, subject: "modern", topic: "Simla Conference and RIN revolt",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Lord Mountbatten was the Viceroy when the Shimla Conference took place.</li><li>Indian Navy Revolt, 1946 took place when the Indian sailors in the Royal Indian Navy at Bombay and Karachi rose against the Government.</li></ol>
Which of the statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the <b>Simla Conference</b> (June–July 1945) was called by Viceroy <b>Lord Wavell</b> (Wavell Plan). Mountbatten became Viceroy only in March 1947.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> the <b>Royal Indian Navy revolt</b> began on 18 February 1946 on HMIS Talwar in <b>Bombay</b> and spread to Karachi and other ports (about 20,000 sailors), over poor food, racial discrimination and the INA trials.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The RIN mutineers surrendered after Patel and Jinnah advised them to; Aruna Asaf Ali supported them. It hastened the Cabinet Mission's arrival.</li>
<li>Other 1945–46 upsurges: INA trials protests, Tebhaga (Bengal), Telangana armed struggle, Punnapra–Vayalar.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2005-148", year: 2005, no: 148, subject: "polity", topic: "Panchayats and municipalities",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Part IX of the Constitution of India contains provisions for Panchayats and was inserted by the Constitution (73rd Amendment) Act, 1992.</li><li>Part IX A of the Constitution of India contains provisions for municipalities and the Article 243Q envisages two types of municipalities: a Municipal Council and a Municipal Corporation for every State.</li></ol>
Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Part IX (Articles 243–243O) on Panchayats was inserted by the <b>73rd Amendment</b> (1992, in force 24 April 1993; National Panchayati Raj Day).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Article 243Q provides for <b>three</b> types: a <b>Nagar Panchayat</b> (transitional area), a <b>Municipal Council</b> (smaller urban area) and a <b>Municipal Corporation</b> (larger urban area). (Part IXA came from the 74th Amendment.)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Common features: one-third seats for women (many states give 50%), reservation for SC/ST, State Election Commission (243K/243ZA), State Finance Commission (243I/243Y), five-year terms.</li>
<li>Schedules 11 (29 subjects for panchayats) and 12 (18 for municipalities). The Supreme Court's triple test (2021) governs OBC reservation in local bodies.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2005-149", year: 2005, no: 149, subject: "polity", topic: "Special provisions and citizenship",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Articles 371A to 371I were inserted in the Constitution of India to meet regional demands of Nagaland, Assam, Manipur, Andhra Pradesh, Sikkim, Mizoram, Arunachal Pradesh and Goa.</li><li>Constitutions of India and the United States of America envisage a dual policy (the Union and the States) but a single citizenship.</li><li>A naturalized citizen of India can never be deprived of his citizenship.</li></ol>
Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 3", "3 only", "1 only"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> 371A Nagaland, 371B Assam, 371C Manipur, 371D–E Andhra Pradesh, 371F Sikkim, 371G Mizoram, 371H Arunachal Pradesh, 371I Goa. (371 covers Maharashtra and Gujarat; 371J, added 2012, covers Karnataka's Hyderabad–Karnataka region.)</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> India has dual polity with <b>single</b> citizenship, but the USA has <b>dual citizenship</b> (federal and state).</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> under Section 10 of the Citizenship Act, 1955, a citizen by registration or naturalisation <b>can be deprived</b> of citizenship (e.g. fraud, disloyalty, trading with the enemy in war, or living abroad 7 years continuously).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Citizenship (Articles 5–11); Citizenship (Amendment) Act 2019 (rules notified March 2024) for persecuted minorities from Pakistan, Afghanistan, Bangladesh. The Supreme Court upheld Section 6A (Assam Accord) in 2024.</li>
<li>OCI cardholders are not citizens; India does not allow dual citizenship.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2005-150", year: 2005, no: 150, subject: "science", topic: "Invention of the laser",
  q: `Who among the following invented lasers?`,
  opts: ["Theodore Maiman", "Denis Papin", "William Morton", "Francis Cricks"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Theodore Maiman</h3>
<p><b>Theodore Maiman</b> built the first working <b>laser</b> (a ruby laser) at Hughes Research Laboratories, California, in May 1960. The theory came from Einstein's stimulated emission (1917) and the maser (Townes, Basov, Prokhorov; Nobel 1964).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Denis Papin invented the <b>pressure cooker</b> ("steam digester", 1679) and the safety valve.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> William Morton first publicly demonstrated <b>ether anaesthesia</b> (1846).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Francis Crick, with James Watson, proposed DNA's double helix (1953).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Laser uses: optical fibre communication, barcode scanners, LASIK, LIDAR, cutting/welding, directed-energy weapons (DRDO's 30 kW laser system tested 2025).</li>
<li>Nobel 2018 (Physics): optical tweezers (Ashkin) and chirped pulse amplification (Mourou, Strickland). Nobel 2023: attosecond laser pulses.</li>
</ul>` }
);
;
