window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2003-106", year: 2003, no: 106, subject: "environment", topic: "Acid rain from thermal power", provKey: "A",
  q: `Assertion (A): Coal based thermal power stations contribute to acid rain.
Reason (R): Oxides of carbon are emitted when the coal burns.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (a). But acid rain comes mainly from <b>sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides</b>, not oxides of carbon, so R does not explain A; examday/dalvoy also give (b).</div>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> coal plants emit SO<sub>2</sub> and NO<sub>x</sub>, which form sulphuric and nitric acids in the air and fall as acid rain (pH below 5.6).</li>
<li><b>R (true):</b> burning coal does release CO<sub>2</sub> and CO. But CO<sub>2</sub> forms only weak carbonic acid, which gives normal rain its pH of about 5.6; it is not the cause of acid rain.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> R is true but is not the reason.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> R is true.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> A is true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Flue-gas desulphurisation (FGD) units cut SO<sub>2</sub>. India's 2015 norms required them, but deadlines kept slipping, and in July 2025 the government exempted most plants away from cities and polluted areas.</li>
<li>Acid rain damages the Taj Mahal's marble ("marble cancer"; Mathura refinery, Taj Trapezium Zone).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-107", year: 2003, no: 107, subject: "science", topic: "Weight and latitude",
  q: `Assertion (A): The weight of a body decreases with the increase of latitude on earth
Reason (R): The earth is not perfect sphere`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) A is false but R is true</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (false):</b> weight <b>increases</b> with latitude. g is about 9.78 m/s² at the equator and 9.83 m/s² at the poles.</li>
<li><b>R (true):</b> the Earth is an oblate spheroid, flattened at the poles. The polar radius is about 21 km less than the equatorial radius, so the poles are closer to the centre and g is higher there. Rotation also lowers effective g at the equator.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> and <b>(b)</b> need A to be true.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> A is false and R is true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mass stays the same everywhere; weight (mg) changes. A spring balance shows the change; a beam balance does not.</li>
<li>See Q79 (g and Earth's radius).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-108", year: 2003, no: 108, subject: "science", topic: "Saturated and unsaturated fats",
  q: `Assertion (A): Unsaturated fats are more reactive compared with the saturated fats.
Reason (R): Unsaturated fats have only single bonds in their structure.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) A is true but R is false</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> the C=C double bonds in unsaturated fats react readily: they oxidise (go rancid) and take up hydrogen.</li>
<li><b>R (false):</b> <b>saturated</b> fats have only single bonds. Unsaturated fats have one (mono-) or more (poly-) double bonds.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> and <b>(b)</b> need R to be true.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> A is true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Hydrogenation turns liquid oils into solid vanaspati, which can create harmful <b>trans fats</b>. FSSAI caps industrial trans fat at 2% of fats and oils (since 2022).</li>
<li>Saturated fats (ghee, coconut oil) are solid at room temperature; unsaturated ones (mustard and sunflower oil) are liquid.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-109", year: 2003, no: 109, subject: "geography", topic: "Moisture and latitude",
  q: `Assertion (A): The amount of moisture in the atmosphere is related to latitude.
Reason (R): The capacity to hold moisture in the form of water vapour is related to temperature.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> water vapour is highest near the equator (up to 4% of air) and falls towards the poles (below 1%).</li>
<li><b>R (true, and the reason):</b> warm air can hold much more water vapour than cold air (about 7% more per °C, the Clausius–Clapeyron relation). Temperature falls with latitude, so moisture does too.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> R does explain A.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> and <b>(d)</b> Both statements are true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The same physics explains why global warming brings heavier downpours: a warmer atmosphere holds more water.</li>
<li>Relative humidity is the share of the maximum possible vapour at that temperature; dew point is the temperature at which air becomes saturated.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-110", year: 2003, no: 110, subject: "environment", topic: "Tropical rainforest soils",
  q: `Assertion (A): Unlike temperate forests, the tropical rain forests, if cleared, can yield productive farm-land that can support intensive agriculture for several years even without chemical fertilizers.
Reason (R): The primary productivity of the tropical rain forest is very high when compared to that of the temperature forests.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) A is false but R is true</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (false):</b> rainforest soils (laterite/oxisols) are poor. Most nutrients are stored in the living plants, and heavy rain leaches the soil, so cleared land loses fertility within a few years. That is why shifting cultivation moves on.</li>
<li><b>R (true):</b> rainforests have the highest primary productivity of any land biome, thanks to warmth, light and water all year.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> and <b>(b)</b> need A to be true.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> A is false.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Temperate forest soils (brown earths) keep more organic matter and are better for farming once cleared.</li>
<li>Shifting cultivation is called <i>jhum</i> in North-East India. Laterite soils are found in Kerala, Karnataka, Odisha and Meghalaya.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-111", year: 2003, no: 111, subject: "modern", topic: "Rowlatt Act resignations",
  q: `Assertion (A): In 1916, Maulana Mohammad Ali and Abul Kalam Azad resigned from the Legislative Council.
Reason (R): The Rowlatt Act was passed by the government in spite of being opposed by all Indian members of the Legislative Council.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) A is false but R is true</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (false):</b> in 1916 Mohammad Ali and Abul Kalam Azad were <b>interned</b> by the government for their pro-Turkey (pan-Islamic) writings; they were not Council members. It was <b>M.A. Jinnah</b> (with Madan Mohan Malaviya and Mazharul Haque) who resigned from the Imperial Legislative Council in <b>1919</b> over the Rowlatt Act.</li>
<li><b>R (true):</b> the Rowlatt Act (March 1919) was passed although every elected Indian member opposed it.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> and <b>(b)</b> need A to be true.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> A is false and R is true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Rowlatt Act allowed detention without trial ("No appeal, no vakil, no daleel"). Gandhi's Rowlatt Satyagraha (April 1919) was his first all-India campaign.</li>
<li>The Ali brothers later led the Khilafat Movement (1919–24).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-112", year: 2003, no: 112, subject: "medieval", topic: "Shah Alam II",
  q: `Assertion (A): Shah Alam II spent the initial years as an Emperor far away from his capital.
Reason (R): There was always a lurking danger of foreign invasion from the north-west frontier.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> Shah Alam II became emperor in 1759 while in exile in Bihar and Awadh. After his defeat at Buxar (1764) he lived under English protection at Allahabad, and only returned to Delhi with Maratha help in 1772.</li>
<li><b>R (true):</b> the north-west was a real threat (Ahmad Shah Abdali's invasions, Panipat 1761).</li>
<li>But he stayed away mainly because Delhi was controlled by hostile powers (the wazir Imad-ul-Mulk, who had murdered his father, and later the Rohilla Najib-ud-Daula), not because of fear of invasion as such.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Debated: some read Abdali's dominance of Delhi as part of the reason and choose (a). The key gives (b).</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> R was not the main reason.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> and <b>(d)</b> Both statements are true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Treaty of Allahabad (1765): Shah Alam granted the <b>Diwani</b> of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa to the East India Company.</li>
<li>He was blinded by the Rohilla Ghulam Qadir (1788).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-113", year: 2003, no: 113, subject: "geography", topic: "Equatorial rainfall",
  q: `Assertion (A): Areas lying within five to eight degrees latitude on either side of the equator receive rainfall throughout the year.
Reason (R): High temperatures and high humidity cause convectional rain to fall mostly in the afternoons near the equator.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> the equatorial belt (about 5–10° N and S: Amazon, Congo, Indonesia) has rain in every month, with no dry season.</li>
<li><b>R (true, and the reason):</b> strong heating makes moist air rise every day, building cumulonimbus clouds and the classic <b>4 o'clock rain</b> with thunderstorms.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> R does explain A.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> and <b>(d)</b> Both statements are true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>This is the zone of the Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ), the "doldrums". Its seasonal shift north drives India's south-west monsoon.</li>
<li>Rainfall types: convectional (equator), orographic (Western Ghats, Cherrapunji–Mawsynram), cyclonic or frontal (temperate lands).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-114", year: 2003, no: 114, subject: "medieval", topic: "Saluva Narasimha",
  q: `Assertion (A): Saluva Narasimha put an end to the old dynasty and assumed the royal title.
Reason (R): He wanted to save the kingdom from further degeneration and disintegration.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> in 1485 Saluva Narasimha, a powerful chief, deposed the weak last Sangama ruler (Virupaksha Raya II's line) and founded the <b>Saluva dynasty</b>, the "first usurpation" of Vijayanagara.</li>
<li><b>R (true, and the reason):</b> the late Sangamas had lost territory to the Bahmanis and Gajapatis, and he seized power to stop the kingdom breaking up.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> R does explain A.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> and <b>(d)</b> Both statements are true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Then came Narasa Nayaka and Vira Narasimha (the Tuluva "second usurpation", 1505), followed by Krishnadeva Raya (1509–29). See 2004 Q2 on the four dynasties.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-115", year: 2003, no: 115, subject: "modern", topic: "Rise of the Marathas",
  q: `Assertion (A): Marathas emerged as the strongest native power in India after the decline of Mughal empire.
Reason (R): Marathas were the first to have clear concept of a united Indian nation.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) A is true but R is false</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> by the mid-18th century, the Marathas under the Peshwas (Baji Rao I, Balaji Baji Rao) collected <i>chauth</i> and <i>sardeshmukhi</i> from Delhi to Tanjore and were the strongest Indian power.</li>
<li><b>R (false):</b> they were a confederacy pursuing regional and dynastic interests. A clear idea of a united Indian nation came only with modern nationalism in the 19th century.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> and <b>(b)</b> need R to be true.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> A is true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Maratha decline: Panipat (1761) and three Anglo-Maratha Wars (ending 1818). The confederacy: Peshwa (Pune), Bhonsle (Nagpur), Gaekwad (Baroda), Holkar (Indore), Scindia (Gwalior).</li>
<li>The Maratha Military Landscapes (12 forts) became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in July 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-116", year: 2003, no: 116, subject: "geography", topic: "Caspian Sea littoral states",
  q: `Which one of the following countries does NOT border the Caspian Sea?`,
  opts: ["Armenia", "Azerbaijan", "Kazakhstan", "Turkmenistan"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Armenia</h3>
<p>The <b>Caspian Sea</b> is bordered by Russia, Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan, Iran and Azerbaijan. <b>Armenia</b> is landlocked and lies west of Azerbaijan.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Azerbaijan (capital Baku) is on the western shore.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Kazakhstan is on the north-east shore.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Turkmenistan is on the east shore.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Caspian is the world's largest inland body of water. The Convention on its Legal Status (Aktau, 2018) was signed by all five states.</li>
<li>Armenia and Azerbaijan initialled a US-brokered peace deal in August 2025, including the "Trump Route" (TRIPP) corridor through Armenia's Syunik. The INSTC route links India to Russia via Iran and the Caspian.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-117", year: 2003, no: 117, subject: "ca", caYear: 2003, topic: "Soma Biswas",
  q: `The sportsperson Soma Biswas is associated with`,
  opts: ["Sailing", "Hockey", "Golf", "Athletics"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Athletics</h3>
<p><b>Soma Biswas</b> (West Bengal) is a heptathlete. She won silver at the 2002 Busan Asian Games and the 2006 Doha Asian Games, and received the Arjuna Award.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> She was not a sailor.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> She did not play hockey.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> She was not a golfer.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The heptathlon has seven events: 100 m hurdles, high jump, shot put, 200 m, long jump, javelin and 800 m. Swapna Barman won India's first Asian Games heptathlon gold (2018).</li>
<li>Neeraj Chopra (javelin) won Olympic gold in 2021 and silver in 2024.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-118", year: 2003, no: 118, subject: "environment", topic: "Photochemical smog",
  q: `Which one of following is produced during the formation of photochemical smog?`,
  opts: ["Hydrocarbons", "Nitrogen Oxides", "Ozone", "Methane"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Ozone</h3>
<p>In sunlight, nitrogen oxides and volatile hydrocarbons from vehicles react to <b>produce</b> secondary pollutants: <b>ground-level ozone</b>, PAN (peroxyacetyl nitrate) and aldehydes. This brownish haze is photochemical smog (as in Los Angeles).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Hydrocarbons are inputs (precursors), not products.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Nitrogen oxides are also precursors.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Methane is not a key product of the reaction.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>"Classical" London smog (1952) was coal smoke plus SO<sub>2</sub> and fog. Delhi's winter smog mixes both types with stubble-burning smoke.</li>
<li>Ozone is one of the 8 pollutants in India's Air Quality Index. NCAP aims for a 40% cut in PM10 by 2025–26 in 131 cities.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-119", year: 2003, no: 119, subject: "culture", topic: "Kitab-i-Nauras and Qawwali",
  q: `Consider the following statement:
<ol><li>Kitab-I-Nauras, a collection of songs in praise of Hindu deities and Muslim saints, was written by Ibrahim Adil Shah II</li><li>Amir Khusrau was the originator in India of the early form of the musical style known as Qawwali.</li></ol>
Which of these statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["Only 1", "Only 2", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> <i>Kitab-i-Nauras</i> by Ibrahim Adil Shah II of Bijapur (1580–1627) is a book of songs in Dakhni praising Saraswati, Ganesha and Sufi saints. He was called "Jagadguru" for his tolerance.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Amir Khusrau (1253–1325), a disciple of Nizamuddin Auliya, is credited with shaping <b>qawwali</b> and with the <i>tarana</i> style; the sitar and tabla are traditionally credited to him too.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Khusrau served several Delhi Sultans and wrote in Persian and Hindavi; he was called the "Tuti-i-Hind" (Parrot of India).</li>
<li>Bijapur's Gol Gumbaz is Muhammad Adil Shah's tomb.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-121", year: 2003, no: 121, subject: "ca", caYear: 2003, topic: "Women business leaders (2003)",
  q: `Match List I (Distinguished Lady) with List II (Organisation/Industry) and select the correct answer using the codes given below the Lists:
<table><tr><th>List I (Distinguished lady)</th><th>List II (Organisation/industry)</th></tr>
<tr><td>A. Amrita</td><td>1. National Dairy Development Board</td></tr>
<tr><td>B. Anu Aga</td><td>2. Park Hotels</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. Mallika Srinivasan</td><td>3. Pfizer Limited</td></tr>
<tr><td>D. Priya Paul</td><td>4. Thermax Limited</td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td>5. Tractors and Farm Equipment Limited</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["A-1, B-4, C-5, D-2", "A-2, B-4, C-1, D-5", "A-1, B-3, C-5, D-2", "A-2, B-3, C-1, D-5"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) A-1, B-4, C-5, D-2</h3>
<table><tr><th>Person</th><th>Organisation</th></tr>
<tr><td>Amrita Patel</td><td>Chairperson, National Dairy Development Board (1998–2014)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anu Aga</td><td>Thermax (energy and environment engineering)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mallika Srinivasan</td><td>TAFE (Tractors and Farm Equipment); later chair of PESB</td></tr>
<tr><td>Priya Paul</td><td>Park Hotels (Apeejay Surrendra group)</td></tr></table>
<div class="note">The source prints just "Amrita"; it refers to Amrita Patel.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Puts Amrita at Park Hotels and Mallika at NDDB; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Puts Anu Aga at Pfizer; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong on all four.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>NDDB (Anand, 1965; Verghese Kurien) ran Operation Flood. India is the world's largest milk producer (about 24% of world output).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-122", year: 2003, no: 122, subject: "polity", topic: "Presidential electoral college",
  q: `Consider the following statements: In the electoral college for Presidential Election in India.
<ol><li>The value of the vote of an elected Member of Legislative Assembly equals</li><li>The value of the vote of an elected Member of parliament equals</li><li>There were more than 5000 members in the latest election</li></ol>
Which of these statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "Only 2", "1 and 3", "Only 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Only 2</h3>
<div class="note">The Education Province copy drops the formulas that complete statements 1 and 2. examday's copy gives statement 1 as (state population) ÷ (elected MLAs × 1000) and statement 2 as a ratio over the number of elected MPs. The key (b) treats only 2 as correct; statement 1 fails because it does not use the 1971 census population.</div>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong as given):</b> the correct value of an MLA's vote = (state population ÷ number of elected MLAs) ÷ 1,000, using the <b>1971</b> census population (frozen until after the first census after 2026).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> the value of an elected MP's vote = total value of all MLAs' votes ÷ total number of elected MPs (Article 55). It was 708 in 2002 and 700 in 2022, after J&K's assembly was dissolved.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> the 2002 electoral college had about 4,896 members (776 elected MPs and 4,120 elected MLAs), fewer than 5,000.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nominated MPs and MLCs do not vote. Members from the Delhi and Puducherry assemblies vote (70th Amendment, 1992). Voting uses the single transferable vote by secret ballot.</li>
<li>President Droupadi Murmu was elected in 2022; the next election is due in 2027.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-123", year: 2003, no: 123, subject: "medieval", topic: "18th-century rulers",
  q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?`,
  opts: ["Ali Mardan Khan introduced the i system of revenue farming in Bengal", "Maharaja Ranjit Singh set up modern foundries to manufacture cannons at Lahore", "Sawai Jai Singh of Amber had Euedil's 'Elements of Colometry' translated into Sanskrit", "Sultan Tipu of Mysore gave money for the construction of the idol of Goddess Sarda in the Sringeri temple"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Ali Mardan Khan introduced the i system of revenue farming in Bengal</h3>
<p>Revenue farming (<i>ijaradari</i>) in Bengal was introduced by <b>Murshid Quli Khan</b> (Nawab, 1717–27). Ali Mardan Khan was a Persian noble of Shah Jahan, known for canals such as the one to Delhi.</p>
<div class="note">The source's typos are kept: "the i system" (the ijara system) and "Euedil's 'Elements of Colometry'" (Euclid's <i>Elements of Geometry</i>).</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> True: Ranjit Singh set up cannon foundries at Lahore and modernised his army with European officers (Ventura, Allard).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> True: Sawai Jai Singh II had Euclid's <i>Elements</i> translated into Sanskrit (as <i>Rekhaganita</i>). He also built the Jantar Mantar observatories at Delhi, Jaipur, Ujjain, Mathura and Varanasi.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> True: Tipu Sultan funded the consecration of the Goddess Sharada idol at Sringeri after the Maratha raid of 1791.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Jaipur's Jantar Mantar is a UNESCO World Heritage Site (2010), as is the Jaipur walled city (2019).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-124", year: 2003, no: 124, subject: "polity", topic: "Amendments raising Lok Sabha seats",
  q: `Which of the following Constitutional Amendments are related to raising the number of Members of Lok Sabha to be: elected from the States?`,
  opts: ["6th and 22nd", "13th and 38th", "7th and 31st", "11th and 42nd"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 7th and 31st</h3>
<p>The <b>7th Amendment (1956)</b> rewrote Article 81 after the states were reorganised (500 seats from states). The <b>31st Amendment (1973)</b> raised the maximum elected from states from 500 to <b>525</b> (with 20 from UTs, making 545 with 2 nominated Anglo-Indians).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> 6th (1956): taxes on inter-state sales. 22nd (1969): autonomous state of Meghalaya within Assam.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> 13th (1962): Nagaland. 38th (1975): Emergency-related changes.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> 11th (1961): Vice-President's election by an electoral college. 42nd (1976): "mini Constitution", which froze seats on the 1971 census.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The 84th Amendment (2001) extended the seat freeze until after the first census after 2026. Delimitation must follow that census, and the women's reservation law (106th Amendment, 2023) also takes effect after it.</li>
<li>The new Parliament building (2023) has room for 888 Lok Sabha members.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-125", year: 2003, no: 125, subject: "medieval", topic: "Death of Qutb-ud-din Aibak",
  q: `How did Sultan Qutb-ud-din Aibak die?`,
  opts: ["He was treacherously stabbed to death by one of his ambitious nobles", "He was killed in a battle with Taj-u-din Yildiz, the ruler of Ghazni who entered into a contest with him over the capture of Punjab", "He sustained injuries while besieging the fortress of Kalinjar in Bundelkhand and succumbed to death later", "He died after a fall from his horse while playing Chaugan"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) He died after a fall from his horse while playing Chaugan</h3>
<p><b>Qutb-ud-din Aibak</b> (1206–10) died in Lahore in 1210 after falling from his horse while playing <b>chaugan</b> (polo).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> He was not murdered.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Yildiz was later defeated by Iltutmish at Tarain (1216).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Aibak took Kalinjar (1202), but that did not kill him. Sher Shah Suri died in an explosion while besieging Kalinjar (1545).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Aibak, a slave of Muhammad Ghori, founded the Mamluk (Slave) dynasty. He was called <i>Lakh Baksh</i> for his generosity, began the Qutb Minar, and built the Quwwat-ul-Islam mosque (Delhi) and the Adhai Din ka Jhonpra (Ajmer).</li>
<li>Iltutmish completed the Qutb Minar.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-126", year: 2003, no: 126, subject: "science", topic: "Planetary orbital periods",
  q: `Among the following which planet takes maximum time for one revolution around the Sun?`,
  opts: ["Earth", "Jupiter", "Mars", "Venus"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Jupiter</h3>
<p>The farther a planet is from the Sun, the longer its year (Kepler's third law: T² ∝ a³). <b>Jupiter</b>, the farthest of the four, takes about <b>11.9 Earth years</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Earth: 365.25 days.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Mars: about 687 days.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Venus: about 225 days (its day, 243 Earth days, is longer than its year).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Neptune takes about 165 years. Mercury has the shortest year (88 days).</li>
<li>Saturn has the most known moons (274 recognised in 2025). NASA's Europa Clipper (launched 2024) will reach Jupiter in 2030.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-127", year: 2003, no: 127, subject: "culture", topic: "World languages by speakers",
  q: `Which one among the following languages has largest number of speakers in the world?`,
  opts: ["Bengali", "French", "Japanese", "Portuguese"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Bengali</h3>
<p><b>Bengali</b> (about 23–28 crore speakers in Bangladesh and India) has more speakers than Portuguese (about 26 crore in total, mostly in Brazil), French and Japanese (about 12.5 crore).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> French has about 8 crore native speakers (about 30 crore in total).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Japanese is spoken almost only in Japan.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Portuguese is close but below Bengali.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bengali is about the 7th most spoken language in the world. Its Language Movement (21 February 1952) is marked as International Mother Language Day (UNESCO).</li>
<li>Bengali, Marathi, Pali, Prakrit and Assamese became classical languages in October 2024 (11 in total).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-128", year: 2003, no: 128, subject: "modern", topic: "Charter Act of 1833",
  q: `Which one of the following provisions was NOT made in the Charter Act of 1833?`,
  opts: ["The trading activities of the East India Company were to be abolished", "The designation of the supreme authority was to be changed as the Governor General of India in Council", "All law-making powers to be conferred on Governor General in Council", "An Indian was to be appointed as a Law Member in the Governor­ General's Council"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) An Indian was to be appointed as a Law Member in the Governor­ General's Council</h3>
<p>The Charter Act 1833 added a <b>fourth (law) member</b> to the Council, but no Indian was required or appointed. The first was <b>Thomas Babington Macaulay</b>. The first Indian in the Executive Council was S.P. Sinha (Law Member, 1909).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Made: the Company lost its China tea monopoly and became a purely administrative body.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Made: the Governor-General of Bengal became the <b>Governor-General of India</b> (Bentinck was the first).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Made: law-making was centralised in the Governor-General in Council; Bombay and Madras lost their legislative powers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Act declared that no Indian should be barred from office by religion, birth or colour (section 87), and set up a Law Commission (Macaulay), which drafted the IPC.</li>
<li>The Charter Act 1853 introduced open competition for the civil services. The IPC was replaced by the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita in July 2024.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-129", year: 2003, no: 129, subject: "science", topic: "Gut hormones",
  q: `With reference to normal human beings consider the following statements:
<ol><li>In response to the presence of HCL, secretian is produced from the duodenum</li><li>Enterogastrone is produced in the small intestine in response to the presence of fatty acids</li></ol>
Which of these statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["Only 1", "Only 2", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> acid (HCl) from the stomach entering the duodenum triggers <b>secretin</b> (the first hormone discovered, by Bayliss and Starling, 1902). It makes the pancreas release bicarbonate. "Secretian" in the source is a typo.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> fats in the small intestine trigger <b>enterogastrone</b> (now understood to include GIP and CCK), which slows gastric emptying and acid secretion.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Gastrin (stomach) increases acid; CCK releases bile and pancreatic enzymes. GLP-1, another gut hormone, is the target of weight-loss drugs (semaglutide, tirzepatide), which reached India in 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-130", year: 2003, no: 130, subject: "geography", topic: "Survey of India",
  q: `Survey of India is under the Ministry of`,
  opts: ["Defence", "Environment and forests", "Home Affairs", "Science and Technology"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Science and Technology</h3>
<p>The <b>Survey of India</b> (1767, India's oldest scientific department, headquartered at Dehradun) is under the <b>Department of Science and Technology</b>, Ministry of Science and Technology. It is the national mapping agency.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Defence has its own military survey units, but not the Survey of India.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Environment oversees the Forest Survey of India and Botanical and Zoological Surveys.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Home Affairs runs the Registrar General and Census, not mapping.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Great Trigonometrical Survey (Lambton, Everest) measured Peak XV (Everest, 1856). The National Geospatial Policy (2022) freed most map data for open use.</li>
<li>SVAMITVA (drone survey of village homes, with Survey of India) has issued over 2 crore property cards.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2003-131", year: 2003, no: 131, subject: "ir", topic: "Women's suffrage",
  q: `Which among the following countries was the earliest to give women the right to vote?`,
  opts: ["Iceland", "India", "New Zealand", "USA"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) New Zealand</h3>
<p><b>New Zealand</b> (1893) was the first self-governing country to give all women the vote in national elections, after Kate Sheppard's campaign.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Iceland: 1915.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> India: universal adult franchise from 1950 (some propertied women could vote in provinces from the 1920s).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> USA: 1920 (19th Amendment).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Others: Australia (1902), Finland (1906; first to let women stand for parliament), UK (1918 partial, 1928 equal), Switzerland (1971 federal), Saudi Arabia (2015, local polls).</li>
<li>In India's 2024 Lok Sabha election, women's turnout (65.8%) was higher than men's.</li>
</ul>` }
);
;
