window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2002-30", year: 2002, no: 30, subject: "medieval", topic: "Sufi saints and orders",
  q: `With reference to Sufism in Indian history, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Shaikh Ahmad Sarhandi was a contemporary of Ibrahim Lodi</li><li>Shaikh Nasiruddin Chirag-i-Dehlavi was a disciple of Shaikh Nizamuddin Auliya</li><li>Aurangzeb was contemporary of Shaikh Salim Chisti</li><li>The Qadiri order of Sufis was first introduced in India by Shaikh Niamtullah and Makhdum Muhammad Jilani</li></ol>
Which of these statements are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "1 and 3", "2 and 3", "2 and 4"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2 and 4</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Shaikh Ahmad Sirhindi (1564–1624, Naqshbandi, "Mujaddid Alf Sani") lived under <b>Akbar and Jahangir</b>, long after Ibrahim Lodi (died 1526).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Nasiruddin Chiragh-i-Dehli was Nizamuddin Auliya's chief disciple and successor in Delhi.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Shaikh Salim Chishti (died 1572) was <b>Akbar's</b> contemporary; Jahangir (Salim) was named after him, and his tomb is at Fatehpur Sikri.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> the Qadiri order (founded by Abdul Qadir Jilani of Baghdad) was brought to India in the 15th century by Shah Niamatullah and Makhdum Muhammad Jilani. Dara Shikoh was a Qadiri.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Main orders: Chishti (Muinuddin, Ajmer; Nizamuddin, Delhi), Suhrawardi (Multan), Naqshbandi (orthodox; Baqi Billah, Sirhindi), Qadiri, Firdausi (Bihar).</li>
<li>The Ajmer Sharif Urs is the largest Sufi gathering. Sufi practices: <i>sama</i> (music), <i>khanqah</i> (hospice), <i>pir–murid</i> (master–disciple).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-31", year: 2002, no: 31, subject: "polity", topic: "High Court judges' salaries",
  q: `The salaries and allowances of the Judges of the High Court are charged do the`,
  opts: ["Consolidated Fund of India", "Consolidated Fund of the State", "Contingency Fund of India", "Contingency Fund of the State"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Consolidated Fund of the State</h3>
<p>High Court judges' salaries and allowances are <b>charged on the Consolidated Fund of the State</b> (Article 202(3)(d)), so the state legislature does not vote on them. Their <b>pensions</b> are charged on the Consolidated Fund of <b>India</b> (Article 112(3)(d)(iii)).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Consolidated Fund of India pays Supreme Court judges' salaries and High Court judges' pensions, not their salaries.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> and <b>(d)</b> Contingency Funds are only for urgent, unforeseen spending, to be repaid later.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Judges' pay is set by the High Court and Supreme Court Judges (Salaries and Conditions of Service) Act, 1954 (revised in 2018). The Supreme Court ruled in 2025 on equal pension for all retired High Court judges (One Rank One Pension).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-32", year: 2002, no: 32, subject: "science", topic: "Propagation by stem cuttings",
  q: `Consider the following plants:
<ol><li>Bougainvillea</li><li>Carnations</li><li>Cocoa</li><li>Grapes</li></ol>
Which of these plants are propagated by stem cuttings?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "2, 3 and 4", "1, 3 and 4", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3>
<h3>Plant by plant</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 Bougainvillea:</b> commonly grown from hardwood stem cuttings.</li>
<li><b>2 Carnations:</b> grown from terminal stem cuttings.</li>
<li><b>3 Cocoa:</b> usually raised from seed or by budding and grafting, but rooted stem cuttings are also used for clonal plants.</li>
<li><b>4 Grapes:</b> grown from hardwood stem cuttings.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Debated: cocoa is more often grown from seed or grafted, so some would leave it out, but no option lists only 1, 2 and 4.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other vegetative methods: layering (jasmine), grafting (mango, citrus), budding (rose), tubers (potato), rhizomes (ginger), bulbs (onion), tissue culture (banana, orchids).</li>
<li>India grows cocoa mainly in Kerala, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-33", year: 2002, no: 33, subject: "polity", topic: "Constituent Assembly composition",
  q: `The members of the Constituent Assembly which drafted the Constitution of India were`,
  opts: ["nominated by the British Parliament", "nominated by the Governor General", "elected by the Legislative Assemblies of various provinces", "elected by the Indian National Congress and Muslim League"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) elected by the Legislative Assemblies of various provinces</h3>
<p>Under the Cabinet Mission Plan, the 292 members for British India were <b>indirectly elected</b> (July 1946) by the provincial legislative assemblies using the single transferable vote, in separate Muslim, Sikh and General seats. The 93 members for the princely states were nominated by their rulers.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The British Parliament nominated no one.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Governor-General did not nominate members.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Parties contested, but the provincial legislatures did the electing.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Assembly first met on 9 December 1946 (Sachchidananda Sinha as interim president; Rajendra Prasad elected permanent president). It adopted the Constitution on 26 November 1949 (Constitution Day).</li>
<li>It had 15 women members, including Sarojini Naidu, Hansa Mehta and Ammu Swaminathan.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-35", year: 2002, no: 35, subject: "culture", topic: "Mudrarakshasa",
  q: `The ancient Indian play Mudra rakshasa of Visakhadutt has its subject on`,
  opts: ["a conflict between Gods and Demons of ancient Hindu lore", "a romantic story of an Aryan prince and a tribal woman", "the story of the power struggle between two Aryan tribes", "the court intrigues at the time of Chandragupta Maurya"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) the court intrigues at the time of Chandragupta Maurya</h3>
<p>Vishakhadatta's <i>Mudrarakshasa</i> ("The Signet Ring of Rakshasa") shows <b>Chanakya</b> outwitting <b>Rakshasa</b>, the loyal minister of the defeated Nandas, and winning him over to serve Chandragupta Maurya.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Conflicts between gods and demons belong to the Puranas.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> No such romance is its theme.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Not a struggle between tribes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Vishakhadatta (Gupta period) also wrote <i>Devichandraguptam</i> (Chandragupta II and the Shakas). See 2003 Q42 on <i>Mrichchhakatika</i>.</li>
<li>Sources on the Mauryas: Kautilya's <i>Arthashastra</i>, Megasthenes' <i>Indica</i>, Ashoka's edicts, the Junagadh inscription of Rudradaman.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-36", year: 2002, no: 36, subject: "economy", topic: "External debt 2000–01", provKey: "A",
  q: `India's external debt increased from US $98,158 million as at the end of March 2000 to US $100,225 million as at the end of March 2001 due to increase in`,
  opts: ["multilateral and bilateral debt", "rupee debt", "commercial borrowings and NRI deposits", "borrowing from International Monetary Fund"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) commercial borrowings and NRI deposits</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (a). But RBI data show <b>multilateral debt fell</b> in 2000–01 (about $31.4 billion to $31.1 billion). The rise came from the <b>India Millennium Deposits</b> (about $5.5 billion raised from NRIs, October–November 2000) and commercial borrowings.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Multilateral debt fell and bilateral debt rose little.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Rupee debt (to the former USSR) was being paid down.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> India had repaid the IMF; outstanding IMF credit was nil.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's external debt was about $736 billion at end-March 2025 (about 19% of GDP). Commercial borrowings are the largest part, and the US dollar is the main currency (about 54%).</li>
<li>Earlier NRI bond issues: Resurgent India Bonds (1998), after the Pokhran sanctions.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-37", year: 2002, no: 37, subject: "economy", topic: "Sectoral shares of GDP 2000–01", provKey: "C",
  q: `Which reference to the Indian economy, consider the following activities:
<ol><li>Agriculture, Forestry and Fishing</li><li>Manufacturing</li><li>Trade, Hotels Transport and Communication</li><li>Financing, Insurance, Real Estate and Business services</li></ol>
the decreasing order of the contribution of these sectors to the Gross Domestic Product (GDP) at factor cost at constant prices (2000-01) is`,
  opts: ["3, 1, 2, 4", "1, 3, 4, 2", "3, 4, 1, 2", "1, 3, 2, 4"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 3, 2, 4</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (c), which puts trade and finance ahead of agriculture. RBI and Economic Survey data (1993–94 prices) for 2000–01 give agriculture and allied about 24.2%, trade, hotels, transport and communication about 22.3%, manufacturing about 17.2%, and finance, insurance and real estate about 12.6%.</div>
<h3>Sector by sector (2000–01 share of GDP)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 Agriculture, forestry and fishing:</b> about 24.2%, the largest.</li>
<li><b>3 Trade, hotels, transport and communication:</b> about 22.3%, second.</li>
<li><b>2 Manufacturing:</b> about 17.2%, third.</li>
<li><b>4 Financing, insurance, real estate and business services:</b> about 12.6%, fourth.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Puts trade above agriculture; wrong for 2000–01 (trade overtook agriculture a few years later).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Puts finance above manufacturing; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Puts agriculture third; wrong.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Now (2024–25, GVA): services about 55%, industry about 27% (manufacturing about 17%), agriculture and allied about 17–18%, though agriculture still employs about 46% of workers.</li>
<li>The GDP base year is being revised from 2011–12 to 2022–23 (new series from February 2026).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-38", year: 2002, no: 38, subject: "ca", caYear: 2002, topic: "Women's pole vault record (2001)",
  q: `Who among the following set a new national record in the women's Pole vault event in the 7th edition of Senior Federation Cup held in Karnataka in the year 2001?`,
  opts: ["G G Pramila", "Jyothna Deka", "Karamjeet Kaur", "Manisha Dey"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Karamjeet Kaur (as per key)</h3>
<p>Per the circulated key, <b>Karamjeet Kaur</b> set the national women's pole vault record at the 2001 Federation Cup (Bangalore). Pole vault was then a new event for Indian women.</p>
<div class="note">Unverified: no independent record of this 2001 mark was found online; the answer follows the key.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> G.G. Pramila was a heptathlete (Asian Games 2002).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> and <b>(d)</b> Not linked to the 2001 pole vault record in the sources checked.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Women's pole vault was added to world championships only in 1999; Indian women have since raised the national record above 4 m.</li>
<li>The men's world record holder Armand Duplantis (Sweden) kept raising it, to 6.30 m (World Championships, Tokyo, September 2025).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-39", year: 2002, no: 39, subject: "ir", topic: "Zimbabwe land crisis",
  q: `The main reason for unrest and violence in Zimbabwe in recent years is due to`,
  opts: ["conflict between different native ethnic groups", "prolonged crisis over land reforms", "economic crisis due to continuous drought and famine", "power struggle between political groups of white European settlers and native black community"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) prolonged crisis over land reforms</h3>
<p>From 2000, President Robert Mugabe's <b>"fast-track" land reform</b> seized white-owned commercial farms, often violently, through "war veterans". This led to farm invasions, deaths, the collapse of farm output, hyperinflation (peaking in 2008) and sanctions.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Shona–Ndebele tensions (the Gukurahundi massacres of the 1980s) were not the issue around 2002.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Drought made the food crisis worse but did not cause the unrest.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> White settlers were not organised political rivals; the struggle was over land.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mugabe was ousted in 2017 and died in 2019. Emmerson Mnangagwa is President. Zimbabwe launched a new gold-backed currency, the ZiG, in April 2024.</li>
<li>In 2020, Zimbabwe agreed to pay $3.5 billion in compensation to white farmers.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-40", year: 2002, no: 40, subject: "culture", topic: "Artists and their arts", provKey: "C",
  q: `Match List I (Artist) with List II (Art) and select the correct answer using the codes given below the lists:
<table><tr><th>List I (Artist)</th><th>List II (Art)</th></tr>
<tr><td>A. Hiren Bhattacharya</td><td>1. Bharatanatyam dance</td></tr>
<tr><td>B. Malini Rajurkar</td><td>2. Hindustani vocal music</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. Pratibha Prahlad</td><td>3. Kuchipudi dance</td></tr>
<tr><td>D. Vempati Chinna Satyam</td><td>4. Puppetry</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3", "A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4", "A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3", "A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (c), which makes Malini Rajurkar a Bharatanatyam dancer and Pratibha Prahlad a singer; that is the wrong way round.</div>
<table><tr><th>Artist</th><th>Art</th></tr>
<tr><td>Hiren Bhattacharya (Assam)</td><td>Puppetry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Malini Rajurkar (Gwalior gharana)</td><td>Hindustani vocal (khayal, tappa)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pratibha Prahlad</td><td>Bharatanatyam</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vempati Chinna Satyam</td><td>Kuchipudi (he reformed the style; died 2012)</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Makes Bhattacharya a Kuchipudi dancer and Vempati a puppeteer; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Swaps Rajurkar and Prahlad; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Makes Bhattacharya a Kuchipudi dancer; wrong.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Malini Rajurkar died in 2023. India's puppetry traditions include Kathputli (Rajasthan), Tholu Bommalata (Andhra), Togalu Gombeyata (Karnataka) and Putul Nach (Bengal).</li>
<li>The Sangeet Natak Akademi recognises eight classical dances (Bharatanatyam, Kathak, Kathakali, Kuchipudi, Odissi, Manipuri, Mohiniyattam, Sattriya), and the Ministry of Culture adds Chhau.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-41", year: 2002, no: 41, subject: "science", topic: "Mirage",
  q: `Consider the following natural phenomena:
<ol><li>Terrestrial heating</li><li>Reflection of light</li><li>Refraction of light</li><li>Diffraction of light</li></ol>
Due to which of these phenomena is mirage formed?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "2, 3 and 4", "1 and 3", "4 only"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3</h3>
<h3>Item by item</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 Terrestrial heating (yes):</b> hot ground heats the air just above it, making it less dense than the air higher up.</li>
<li><b>3 Refraction (yes):</b> light from the sky bends gradually through these layers and finally turns upward (total internal reflection at a grazing angle), so the sky looks like water on the road.</li>
<li><b>2 Reflection (no):</b> no surface reflects the light; the "reflection" is the end stage of refraction.</li>
<li><b>4 Diffraction (no):</b> diffraction is not involved.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>In cold regions an inferior mirage is replaced by a superior mirage (looming, Fata Morgana), with objects appearing above the horizon.</li>
<li>Total internal reflection also explains the sparkle of diamonds and optical fibres.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-42", year: 2002, no: 42, subject: "geography", topic: "Hindalco at Renukoot",
  q: `HINDALCO, an aluminium factory located at Renukut owes its site basically to`,
  opts: ["proximity of raw materials", "abundant supply of power", "efficient transport network", "proximity to the market"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) abundant supply of power</h3>
<p>Smelting aluminium (Hall–Héroult electrolysis) needs huge amounts of electricity, about 14–15 MWh per tonne. <b>Hindalco's Renukoot</b> smelter (1962, Sonbhadra, UP) was sited for cheap power from the <b>Rihand dam</b> (Govind Ballabh Pant Sagar) and nearby Singrauli coal. It also has its own Renusagar power plant.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Bauxite comes from Jharkhand and Chhattisgarh, some distance away.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Transport was not the main factor.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Big markets are far away.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other smelters: NALCO (Angul, Odisha; bauxite from Panchpatmali), Vedanta (Jharsuguda), BALCO (Korba).</li>
<li>India is the world's second-largest aluminium producer (after China). Hindalco owns Novelis (USA).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-43", year: 2002, no: 43, subject: "science", topic: "Loss of limbs in snakes",
  q: `In the context of the organic evolution, the loss of limbs of snakes is explained by the phenomenon of`,
  opts: ["use and disuse of organs", "adaptation to living in burrows", "natural selection", "inheritance of acquired characters"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) use and disuse of organs (as per key)</h3>
<p>The classic textbook example of Lamarck's principle of <b>use and disuse</b> is that snakes lost their limbs from disuse while crawling through narrow spaces.</p>
<div class="note">Debated: modern evolutionary biology explains it through <b>natural selection</b> (c), which favoured long, limbless bodies suited to burrowing (a <i>Hox</i>/ZRS gene enhancer change). Lamarckism is rejected today, but school texts use this example for "use and disuse", and the key follows that.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Burrowing was the ecological setting, not the mechanism named here.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The scientifically accepted mechanism, but not the key's answer.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Inheritance of acquired characters is Lamarck's other principle (the giraffe's neck), not the one meant.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pythons and boas still have tiny vestigial hind-limb spurs (evidence for evolution, like the human appendix and tailbone).</li>
<li>Weismann's germ-plasm theory disproved the inheritance of acquired characters (cutting the tails of mice over generations).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-44", year: 2002, no: 44, subject: "polity", topic: "Purpose of DPSPs",
  q: `The purpose of the inclusion of Directive Principles of State Policy in the Indian Constitution is to establish:`,
  opts: ["political democracy", "social democracy", "Gandhian democracy", "social and economic democracy"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) social and economic democracy</h3>
<p>Fundamental Rights secure <b>political</b> democracy; the Directive Principles (Part IV) aim at <b>social and economic</b> democracy, a welfare state. Ambedkar called them "novel features" meant to realise economic democracy.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Political democracy rests mainly on Fundamental Rights and elections.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Only part of the aim.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Some DPSPs are Gandhian (Articles 40, 43, 46, 47, 48), but they are only one strand.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>DPSPs are not enforceable in courts (Article 37), but they are "fundamental in the governance of the country". <i>Minerva Mills</i> (1980) held that a balance between Fundamental Rights and DPSPs is part of the basic structure.</li>
<li>Uttarakhand implemented a Uniform Civil Code (Article 44) in January 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-45", year: 2002, no: 45, subject: "economy", topic: "National Renewal Fund",
  q: `Consider the following statements: The objectives of the National Renewal Funds set up in February 1992 were
<ol><li>to give training and counselling for workers affected by retrenchment or VRS.</li><li>redeployment of workers</li></ol>
Which of these statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["Neither 1 nor 2", "Both 1 and 2", "1 only", "2 only"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the NRF (February 1992) paid for voluntary retirement (VRS) compensation and for counselling and retraining of workers in sick public sector units.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> it also funded the redeployment of displaced workers.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The Education Province copy prints this question twice (as Q45 and Q50). The real Q50 is missing from the source and is skipped.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The NRF was a "social safety net" in the 1991 reforms; it was wound up in 2000. Since 2000, the Department of Public Enterprises has run a counselling, retraining and redeployment scheme.</li>
<li>Labour Codes (in force November 2025): the Industrial Relations Code creates a Worker Re-skilling Fund.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-46", year: 2002, no: 46, subject: "economy", topic: "Small-scale reservation",
  q: `Which one of the following committees recommended the abolition of reservation of items for the small-scale sector in industry?`,
  opts: ["Abid Hussain Committee", "Narasimham Committee", "Nayak Committee", "Rakesh Mohan Committee"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Abid Hussain Committee</h3>
<p>The <b>Abid Hussain Committee</b> on small-scale industries (1997) recommended ending the list of products reserved for small units (then over 800 items), arguing it held back technology and scale.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Narasimham Committees (1991, 1998) dealt with <b>banking</b> reform.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Nayak Committee (1992) dealt with <b>working capital credit</b> for small industries (20% of turnover).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Rakesh Mohan Committee (1996) dealt with <b>infrastructure</b> financing.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reservation was phased out; the last 20 items were de-reserved in 2015.</li>
<li>MSME definitions were revised in 2020 and 2025 (investment up to Rs 2.5 crore for micro, Rs 25 crore for small and Rs 125 crore for medium units).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-47", year: 2002, no: 47, subject: "geography", topic: "Phases of population growth",
  q: `India's population growth during the 29th century can be classified into four distinct phases. Match List I (Period) with List II (Phase) and select the correct answer using the using the codes given below the lists:
<table><tr><th>List I (Period)</th><th>List II (Phase)</th></tr>
<tr><td>A. 1901-21</td><td>1. Steady growth</td></tr>
<tr><td>B. 1921-51</td><td>2. Rapid high growth</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. 1951-81</td><td>3. Stagnant growth</td></tr>
<tr><td>D. 1981-2001</td><td>4. High growth with definite signs of slowdown</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2", "A-1, B-3, C-2, D-4", "A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4", "A-1, B-3, C-4, D-2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4</h3>
<div class="note">"29th century" in the source is a typo for 20th century.</div>
<table><tr><th>Period</th><th>Phase</th></tr>
<tr><td>1901–21</td><td>Stagnant (famines and the 1918 influenza; 1921 is the "Great Divide", the only census showing a fall)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1921–51</td><td>Steady growth (falling death rate)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1951–81</td><td>Population explosion (rapid high growth, about 2.2% a year)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1981–2001</td><td>High growth with clear signs of slowing (falling birth rate)</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Swaps the 1951–81 and 1981–2001 phases; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Makes 1901–21 a period of steady growth; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong on several matches.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's total fertility rate fell below replacement level to 2.0 (NFHS-5, 2019–21). The UN put India at about 146 crore in 2025, the world's most populous country.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-48", year: 2002, no: 48, subject: "culture", topic: "Nargis and the Padma Shri",
  q: `Who was the first Indian actress to receive the Padma Shri Award?`,
  opts: ["Samita Patil", "Nargis Dutt", "Meena Kumari", "Madhubala"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Nargis Dutt</h3>
<p><b>Nargis</b> received the Padma Shri in <b>1958</b>, after <i>Mother India</i> (1957), the first Indian film nominated for the Oscar for Best Foreign Language Film. She was later nominated to the Rajya Sabha (1980).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Smita Patil received the Padma Shri in 1985, much later.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Meena Kumari never received a Padma award.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Madhubala never received a Padma award.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Padma awards were started in 1954 and are announced on Republic Day. Recent film recipients include Mithun Chakraborty (Padma Bhushan 2024) and Ajith Kumar and Shekhar Kapur (Padma Bhushan 2025).</li>
<li>The National Film Award for Best Feature Film on National Integration is named after Nargis Dutt.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-49", year: 2002, no: 49, subject: "medieval", topic: "Sultanate institutions",
  q: `With reference to medieval Indian rulers, which one of the following statements is correct?`,
  opts: ["Alauddin Khalji first set up a separate Ariz's department", "Balban introduced the branding system of horses of military", "Muhammad bin Tughlaq was succeeded by his uncle to the Delhi throne", "Firuz Tughlaq set up a separate department of slaves"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Firuz Tughlaq set up a separate department of slaves</h3>
<p><b>Firuz Shah Tughlaq</b> (1351–88) collected about 1.8 lakh slaves and set up a separate department, the <b>Diwan-i-Bandagan</b>, to manage them.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The army department (<b>Diwan-i-Arz</b>, under the Ariz-i-Mumalik) was set up by <b>Balban</b>.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Branding of horses (<i>dagh</i>) and descriptive rolls of soldiers (<i>huliya</i>) were introduced by <b>Alauddin Khalji</b>.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Muhammad bin Tughlaq was succeeded by his <b>cousin</b> Firuz.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Firuz also set up the Diwan-i-Khairat (charity), Dar-ul-Shifa (hospital) and Diwan-i-Istihqaq (pensions), dug canals (the Western Yamuna canal) and founded Hisar, Jaunpur and Firozabad.</li>
<li>He imposed jizya on Brahmins and made the iqtas hereditary, which weakened the Sultanate.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-51", year: 2002, no: 51, subject: "medieval", topic: "Shah Jahan's Balkh campaign", provKey: "B",
  q: `The motive behind Shahjahan's Balkh campaign was to`,
  opts: ["secure a friendly ruler in Balkh and Badakhshan which bordered Kabul", "conquer Samarqand and Farghana, the Mughal homelands", "fix the Mughal frontier on the 'scientific line', the Amu Daria", "expand the Mughal Empire beyond the sub-continent"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) secure a friendly ruler in Balkh and Badakhshan which bordered Kabul</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (b). The question echoes Satish Chandra's NCERT text almost word for word: Shah Jahan wanted a friendly ruler in Balkh and Badakhshan, which bordered Kabul, because of Uzbek raids. The text says it was wrong to think he aimed at Samarqand and Farghana or the Amu Darya line. Some coaching keys still print (b).</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Mughals spoke of reclaiming their "homelands", but historians reject this as the real motive.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Amu Darya was not a defensible "scientific" frontier.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> No general plan to expand outside the subcontinent.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The campaign (1646–47, under Murad and then Aurangzeb) cost about Rs 4 crore and failed. Qandahar was finally lost to Persia in 1649 despite three sieges.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-52", year: 2002, no: 52, subject: "geography", topic: "HYV wheat and rice",
  q: `Consider the following high yielding varieties of crops in India:
<ol><li>Arjun</li><li>Jaya</li><li>Padma</li><li>Sonalika</li></ol>
Which of these are wheat?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "2 and 3", "1 and 4", "3 and 4"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 4</h3>
<h3>Variety by variety</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 Arjun:</b> a wheat variety.</li>
<li><b>2 Jaya:</b> a rice variety (1968, semi-dwarf).</li>
<li><b>3 Padma:</b> a rice variety.</li>
<li><b>4 Sonalika:</b> a wheat variety (amber grain, from Mexican dwarf wheats).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Green Revolution wheats: Kalyan Sona, Sonalika, Lerma Rojo, Sonora 64 (Norman Borlaug and M.S. Swaminathan). Rice: IR-8 ("miracle rice"), Jaya, Padma.</li>
<li>In 2024, 109 climate-resilient, biofortified varieties were released. India released the world's first genome-edited rice varieties (DRR Dhan 100 "Kamala" and Pusa DST Rice 1) in 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-53", year: 2002, no: 53, subject: "modern", topic: "1875 petition for representation", provKey: "B",
  q: `Which one of the following submitted in 1875 a petition to the House of Commons demanding India's direct representation in the British Parliament?`,
  opts: ["The Deccan Association", "The Indian Association", "The Madras Mahajan Sabha", "The Poona Sarvajanik Sabha"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) The Poona Sarvajanik Sabha</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (b). But the Indian Association was founded only in <b>July 1876</b>, so it could not have petitioned in 1875. The <b>Poona Sarvajanik Sabha</b> (1870; Justice M.G. Ranade) sent the 1875 petition; PMF IAS, civilsdaily and Wikipedia all say so.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Deccan Association was a different body, not linked to this petition.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Indian Association (Surendranath Banerjea and Ananda Mohan Bose, 1876) campaigned for ICS age limits and held the Indian National Conference (1883).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Madras Mahajana Sabha was founded in 1884.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other pre-Congress bodies: British Indian Association (1851), East India Association (Dadabhai Naoroji, London, 1866), Bombay Presidency Association (1885).</li>
<li>Dadabhai Naoroji was the first Indian elected to the House of Commons (1892); his 200th birth anniversary falls in 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-54", year: 2002, no: 54, subject: "modern", topic: "Princes in the 1935 federation",
  q: `The real intention of the British to include the princely states in the Federal Union proposed by the India Act of 1935 was to`,
  opts: ["exercise more and direct political and administrative control over the princely states", "involve the princes actively in the administration of the colony", "finally effect the complete political and administrative take-over of all the princely states by the British", "use the princes to counter-balance the anti-imperialist doctrines of the nationalist leaders"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) use the princes to counter-balance the anti-imperialist doctrines of the nationalist leaders</h3>
<p>Under the 1935 Act, princes were to <b>nominate</b> one-third of the federal Assembly and two-fifths of the Council of State, not elect them. These loyal blocs were meant to outweigh the nationalist members elected from British India.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Act left the states internally autonomous; there was no plan for more direct control.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Princes would sit in the federal legislature but not run British India.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> No takeover was planned; paramountcy continued.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The federation never came into being because not enough princes joined. The provincial part of the Act began in 1937, with Congress ministries in 8 provinces.</li>
<li>Integration of the states after 1947 was led by Sardar Patel and V.P. Menon (Instrument of Accession).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-55", year: 2002, no: 55, subject: "science", topic: "Pleiotropy",
  q: `When one gene control two or more different characters simultaneously, the phenomenon is called`,
  opts: ["Apomixis", "Pleiotropy", "Polyploidy", "Polytony"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Pleiotropy</h3>
<p><b>Pleiotropy</b>: one gene affects several traits. Examples: the sickle-cell gene (anaemia plus malaria resistance) and phenylketonuria (intellectual disability plus lighter pigmentation).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Apomixis is seed formation without fertilisation (asexual seeds, as in some grasses and citrus).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Polyploidy means having more than two sets of chromosomes (common in wheat, banana, cotton).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Not a genetics term here; polygeny (many genes controlling one trait, such as skin colour or height) is the opposite of pleiotropy.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other non-Mendelian patterns: incomplete dominance (snapdragon), co-dominance (ABO blood groups), multiple alleles, epistasis.</li>
<li>Synthetic apomixis is being researched to let farmers save seed from hybrids without losing hybrid vigour.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2002-56", year: 2002, no: 56, subject: "geography", topic: "Solar halo",
  q: `Sun's halo is produced by the refraction of light in`,
  opts: ["Water vapour, in Stratus clouds", "Ice crystals in Cirrocumulus clouds", "Ice crystals in Cirrus clouds", "Dust particles in Stratus clouds"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Ice crystals in Cirrus clouds</h3>
<p>A 22° <b>halo</b> forms when sunlight (or moonlight) is refracted by hexagonal <b>ice crystals</b> in thin, high clouds: cirrus and especially <b>cirrostratus</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Stratus clouds are low water-droplet clouds and do not make halos.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Cirrocumulus clouds are ice-based but lumpy ("mackerel sky"); they make coronas and iridescence rather than full halos.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Dust does not form halos.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>A halo often comes before a warm front and rain. A corona, a smaller coloured ring, comes from diffraction by water droplets.</li>
<li>Cloud families: high (cirrus, cirrostratus, cirrocumulus), middle (alto-), low (stratus, stratocumulus, nimbostratus), vertical (cumulus, cumulonimbus).</li>
</ul>` }
);
;
