window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2001-82", year: 2001, no: 82, subject: "economy", topic: "Indirect taxes",
  q: `Consider the following taxes:
<ol><li>Corporation tax</li><li>Customs duty</li><li>Wealth tax</li><li>Excise duty</li></ol>
Which of these is/are indirect taxes?`,
  opts: ["I only", "II and IV", "I and III", "II and III"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) II and IV</h3>
<h3>Tax by tax</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 Corporation tax:</b> direct (on company profits).</li>
<li><b>2 Customs duty:</b> indirect (on imports and exports; the burden passes to buyers).</li>
<li><b>3 Wealth tax:</b> direct (abolished in 2015).</li>
<li><b>4 Excise duty:</b> indirect (on manufacture; mostly merged into GST in 2017, but still levied on petroleum and tobacco).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Direct taxes now exceed indirect taxes in the Centre's gross tax revenue. GST 2.0 (September 2025) has two main rates (5% and 18%) and a 40% rate for luxury and sin goods; the compensation cess is being wound up.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-83", year: 2001, no: 83, subject: "medieval", topic: "Genghis Khan and Iltutmish",
  q: `The Mongols under Genghis Khan invaded India during the reign of`,
  opts: ["Balban", "Feroze Tughlaq", "Iltutmish", "Muhammad bin Tughlaq"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Iltutmish</h3>
<p>In 1221 <b>Genghis Khan</b> chased the Khwarazmian prince Jalaluddin Mangbarni to the Indus. <b>Iltutmish</b> wisely refused Jalaluddin refuge, and the Mongols turned back without entering the Sultanate.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Balban (1266–87) fought later Mongol raids; his son Prince Muhammad died fighting them (1285).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Firuz Tughlaq (1351–88) faced no Genghis-era invasion.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Muhammad bin Tughlaq faced Tarmashirin's raid (1327), after Genghis's time.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Alauddin Khalji defeated several Mongol invasions (Jaran-Manjur, Kili 1299, Amroha). Timur sacked Delhi in 1398.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-84", year: 2001, no: 84, subject: "geography", topic: "Dense populations of Nile and Java",
  q: `The high density of population in Nile Valley and Island of Java is primarily due to`,
  opts: ["intensive agriculture", "industrialisation", "urbanisation", "topographic constraints"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) intensive agriculture</h3>
<p>Fertile alluvial soils (Nile silt) and volcanic soils (Java), with water all year, support <b>intensive</b> rice and wheat farming that can feed very dense rural populations.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Both regions are mainly agrarian, not industrial.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Urbanisation came later and is not the main reason.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Constraints such as the desert around the Nile concentrate people, but the farming is what supports them.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Java holds about 55% of Indonesia's people on about 7% of its land. Ethiopia's Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Blue Nile was inaugurated in September 2025 over Egypt's objections.</li>
<li>The Ganga plains and Bangladesh's delta are similar dense farming regions.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-85", year: 2001, no: 85, subject: "economy", topic: "FDI by sector 1997–2000",
  q: `The largest share of Foreign Direct Investment (1997-2000) went to`,
  opts: ["Food and food product sector", "Engineering sector", "Electronics and electric equipment sector", "Service sector"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Engineering sector (as per key)</h3>
<p>The Economic Survey's sector-wise data on FDI approvals for 1997–2000 showed <b>engineering</b> (including power, transport equipment and machinery) with the largest share, ahead of services, electronics and food processing.</p>
<div class="note">The share depends on whether approvals or actual inflows, and which sector groupings, are used. The answer follows the key.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Food processing was smaller.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Electronics and electrical equipment was large but behind engineering.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Services became the top sector only later (in the 2000s).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>FDI equity inflows (April 2000–2025): services lead, followed by computer software and hardware, trading, telecom and automobiles. Mauritius and Singapore are the top sources.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-86", year: 2001, no: 86, subject: "polity", topic: "CAG's duties",
  q: `Which one of the following duties is NOT performed by the Comptroller and Auditor General of India?`,
  opts: ["To audit and report on all expenditure from the Consolidated Fund of India", "To audit and report on all expenditure from the Contingency Funds and Public Accounts", "To audit and report on all trading, manufacturing, profit and loss accounts", "To control the receipt and issue of public money, and to ensure that the public revenue is lodged in the exchequer"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) To control the receipt and issue of public money, and to ensure that the public revenue is lodged in the exchequer</h3>
<p>India's CAG is only an <b>auditor</b>. Unlike the UK's Comptroller, the CAG does not control the issue of money from the treasury; the executive releases funds after appropriation.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Performed: audit of the Consolidated Funds (Articles 148–151; CAG Act 1971).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Performed: audit of the Contingency Funds and Public Accounts.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Performed: audit of trading and manufacturing accounts of government departments and PSUs.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>CAG reports go to the President (Union) or Governor (state), then to the PAC. CAG since November 2024: K. Sanjay Murthy. The CAG is removed like a Supreme Court judge, and the term is 6 years or to age 65.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-87", year: 2001, no: 87, subject: "culture", topic: "Art schools and dynasties",
  q: `Which one of the following pairs is correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["Harappan Civilisation ... Painted Grey Ware", "The Kushans ... Gandhara School of Art", "The Mughals ... Ajanta Paintings", "The Marathas ... Pahari School of Painting"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) The Kushans ... Gandhara School of Art</h3>
<p>The <b>Gandhara school</b> (Graeco-Buddhist, grey schist) flourished under the <b>Kushans</b> (Kanishka), producing some of the first images of the Buddha in human form, as did the Mathura school (red sandstone).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Painted Grey Ware belongs to the <b>later Vedic</b> period (about 1100–600 BC), not the Harappans (who made black-on-red ware).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Ajanta paintings are from the Satavahana and Vakataka periods (2nd century BC to 5th century AD), long before the Mughals.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Pahari painting (Basohli, Guler, Kangra) came from Rajput hill states, not the Marathas.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Amaravati school (Satavahanas, white marble) was the third major early school. Ajanta became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1983.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-88", year: 2001, no: 88, subject: "geography", topic: "Sedimentary rocks",
  q: `Consider the following statements made about the sedimentary rocks:
<ol><li>Sedimentary rocks are formed at Earth's surface by the hydrological system.</li><li>The formation of sedimentary rocks involves the weathering of pre-existing rocks.</li><li>Sedimentary rocks contain fossils.</li><li>Sedimentary rocks typically occur in layers.</li></ol>
Which of these statements are correct?`,
  opts: ["I and II", "I and IV", "II, III and IV", "I, II, III and IV"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I, II, III and IV</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> they form at the surface by deposition from water, wind and ice.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> weathering and erosion of older rocks supply the sediment.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> they are the main rocks that hold fossils.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> they are typically stratified (layered).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Examples: sandstone, shale, limestone, coal (organic), rock salt (chemical). They cover about 75% of the land surface but make up only about 5% of the crust by volume.</li>
<li>India's Gondwana coal and Vindhyan sandstone are sedimentary. India's first national geoheritage sites include Lonar crater and others.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-89", year: 2001, no: 89, subject: "economy", topic: "Official publications",
  q: `Match List I with List II and select the correct answer using the codes given below the Lists:
<table><tr><th>List I (Publisher)</th><th>List II (Publication)</th></tr>
<tr><td>I. Ministry of Industry</td><td>A) Report on Currency and Finance</td></tr>
<tr><td>II. Central Statistical Organisation</td><td>B) Economic Survey Statistical Organisation</td></tr>
<tr><td>III. Reserve Bank of India</td><td>C) Wholesale Price Index</td></tr>
<tr><td>IV. Ministry of Finance</td><td>D) National Accounts Statistics</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["I-D, II-C, III-B, IV-A", "I-C, II-D, III-A, IV-B", "I-D, II-C, III-A, IV-B", "I-C, II-D, III-B, IV-A"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) I-C, II-D, III-A, IV-B</h3>
<table><tr><th>Publisher</th><th>Publication</th></tr>
<tr><td>Ministry of Industry (Office of the Economic Adviser)</td><td>Wholesale Price Index</td></tr>
<tr><td>Central Statistical Organisation</td><td>National Accounts Statistics</td></tr>
<tr><td>RBI</td><td>Report on Currency and Finance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ministry of Finance</td><td>Economic Survey</td></tr></table>
<div class="note">Item B is garbled in the source ("Economic Survey Statistical Organisation"); it means the Economic Survey.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Gives the Ministry of Industry the National Accounts; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Same error, and gives the CSO the WPI; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Gives the RBI the Economic Survey; wrong.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The CSO and NSSO merged into the National Statistical Office (NSO, MoSPI) in 2019. The RBI also publishes the Annual Report, the Financial Stability Report and the Handbook of Statistics. The Economic Survey is released the day before the Budget.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-90", year: 2001, no: 90, subject: "ca", caYear: 2001, topic: "Awards and recipients (2000)",
  q: `Match List I with List II and select the correct answer using the codes given below the Lists:
<table><tr><th>List I (Award / Prize)</th><th>List II (Recipient)</th></tr>
<tr><td>I. Indira Gandhi Prize for Peace, Disarmament</td><td>A) Archbishop Desmond Tutu</td></tr>
<tr><td>II. Jamnalal Bajaj Award</td><td>B) Dr. Gurudev Khush</td></tr>
<tr><td>III. International Gandhi Peace Prize</td><td>C) Dr. M.S. Swaminathan</td></tr>
<tr><td>IV. Wolf Prize</td><td>D) Nelson Mandela</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["I-A, II-C, III-D, IV-B", "I-C, II-A, III-D, IV-B", "I-A, II-C, III-B, IV-D", "I-C, II-A, III-B, IV-D"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) I-C, II-A, III-D, IV-B</h3>
<table><tr><th>Award</th><th>Recipient</th></tr>
<tr><td>Indira Gandhi Prize for Peace, Disarmament and Development</td><td>M.S. Swaminathan (1999)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jamnalal Bajaj Award (international, for promoting Gandhian values outside India)</td><td>Desmond Tutu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gandhi Peace Prize</td><td>Nelson Mandela (2000)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wolf Prize in Agriculture</td><td>Gurdev Khush (2000, rice breeding at IRRI)</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Swaps Swaminathan and Tutu; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Swaps Swaminathan and Tutu, and Mandela and Khush; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Swaps Mandela and Khush; wrong.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>M.S. Swaminathan received the Bharat Ratna posthumously (2024). Gurdev Khush (Padma Shri) developed IR36 and IR64 rice. See 2003 Q54 on the Indira Gandhi Prize.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-91", year: 2001, no: 91, subject: "polity", topic: "Fourth Schedule",
  q: `Which one of the following statements correctly describes the Fourth Schedule of the Constitution of India?`,
  opts: ["It lists the distribution of power between the Union and the States", "It contains the languages listed in the Constitution", "It contains the provisions regarding the administration of tribal areas", "It allocates seats in the Council of States"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) It allocates seats in the Council of States</h3>
<p>The <b>Fourth Schedule</b> allocates <b>Rajya Sabha</b> seats to the states and UTs, roughly by population (Uttar Pradesh has the most, 31).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The division of powers is in the <b>Seventh</b> Schedule (Union, State and Concurrent Lists).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The languages are in the <b>Eighth</b> Schedule (22 languages).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Tribal areas are in the <b>Fifth</b> and <b>Sixth</b> Schedules.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Rajya Sabha has 245 seats (233 elected plus 12 nominated); there are 4 seats for J&K. See 2004 Q17.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-92", year: 2001, no: 92, subject: "medieval", topic: "Surat, Babul Makka",
  q: `Which among the following ports was called Babul Makka (Gate of Makka) during the Mughal Period?`,
  opts: ["Calicut", "Broach", "Cambay", "Surat"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Surat</h3>
<p>Under the Mughals, <b>Surat</b> was the main port for Hajj pilgrims sailing to Mecca, hence <b>Bab-ul-Makka</b> ("Gate of Mecca"). It was also the empire's leading trading port, with English (1613) and Dutch factories.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Calicut was the Zamorin's spice port, outside the Mughal empire.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Broach (Bharuch) was an ancient port (Barygaza) that declined.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Cambay (Khambhat) silted up and gave way to Surat.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Shivaji raided Surat in 1664 and 1670. The Surat Diamond Bourse (2023) is among the world's largest office buildings.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-93", year: 2001, no: 93, subject: "geography", topic: "Volcanic seas",
  q: `Volcanic eruptions do not occur in the`,
  opts: ["Baltic Sea", "Black Sea", "Caribbean Sea", "Caspian Sea"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Baltic Sea</h3>
<p>The <b>Baltic Sea</b> lies on the old, stable Baltic (Fennoscandian) Shield, far from plate boundaries, so it has no volcanic activity.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Black Sea region (the Caucasus, Anatolia) has mud volcanoes and past volcanism.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Caribbean has active volcanoes on the Lesser Antilles arc (Montserrat's Soufrière Hills, St Vincent's La Soufrière, 2021).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Caspian has many mud volcanoes (Azerbaijan).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's only active volcano is Barren Island (Andaman). Iceland's Reykjanes peninsula has had repeated eruptions since 2021.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-94", year: 2001, no: 94, subject: "modern", topic: "Hasrat Mohani and complete independence",
  q: `Who among the following leaders proposed to adopt Complete Independence as the goal of the Congress in the Ahmedabad session of 1920?`,
  opts: ["Abul Kalam Azad", "Hasrat Mohani", "Jawaharlal Nehru", "Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Hasrat Mohani</h3>
<p><b>Maulana Hasrat Mohani</b> moved a resolution for <b>complete independence</b> at the Ahmedabad Congress (the session was actually held in December <b>1921</b>; the question says 1920). Gandhi opposed it as premature. Mohani also coined "Inquilab Zindabad".</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Azad was a Khilafat and Congress leader, but did not move this resolution.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Nehru got Purna Swaraj adopted at Lahore (1929).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Gandhi opposed the resolution in 1921.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>See 2004 Q29. The first Independence Day was celebrated on 26 January 1930, which is why the Constitution began on that date in 1950.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-95", year: 2001, no: 95, subject: "ca", caYear: 2001, topic: "Sportswomen 2000–01",
  q: `Match List I with List II and select the correct answer using the codes given below the Lists:
<table><tr><th>List I (Sports Women)</th><th>List II (Sport)</th></tr>
<tr><td>I. Anjali Vedpathak</td><td>A) Athletics</td></tr>
<tr><td>II. Mouma Das</td><td>B) Gymnastics</td></tr>
<tr><td>III. Neelam Singh</td><td>C) Rifle Shooting</td></tr>
<tr><td>IV. Tumpa Debnath</td><td>D) Table Tennis</td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td>E) Chess</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["I-C, II-D, III-B, IV-E", "I-D, II-C, III-A, IV-B", "I-D, II-C, III-B, IV-E", "I-C, II-D, III-A, IV-B"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I-C, II-D, III-A, IV-B</h3>
<table><tr><th>Person</th><th>Sport</th></tr>
<tr><td>Anjali Bhagwat (née Vedpathak)</td><td>Rifle shooting (world No. 1 in 2002)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mouma Das</td><td>Table tennis (Padma Shri 2021)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Neelam J. Singh</td><td>Athletics (discus)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tumpa Debnath</td><td>Gymnastics</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Makes Singh a gymnast and Debnath a chess player; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Makes Bhagwat a table tennis player; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Same error; wrong.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Manu Bhaker won two bronzes at Paris 2024 (the first Indian to win two medals at one Games). Dipa Karmakar, India's best-known gymnast, retired in 2024.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-96", year: 2001, no: 96, subject: "geography", topic: "Marsupials",
  q: `A class of animals known as Marsupials is a characteristic feature of`,
  opts: ["Africa", "Australia", "South America", "South-east Asia"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Australia</h3>
<p><b>Marsupials</b> (pouched mammals such as kangaroos, koalas and wombats) dominate <b>Australia</b>'s mammals because the continent was isolated for so long after it split from Gondwana.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Africa has no native marsupials.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> South America has some (opossums), but they are not its typical feature.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> South-east Asia has none west of the Wallace Line.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Monotremes (egg-laying mammals: the platypus and echidna) are found only in Australia and New Guinea. The Wallace Line separates Asian and Australian fauna.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-98", year: 2001, no: 98, subject: "medieval", topic: "Sultanate departments",
  q: `Which of the following pairs is correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["Dewan-i-bandagani ... Tughlaq", "Dewan-i-Mustakhraj ... Balban", "Dewan-i-Kohi ... Alauddin Khilji", "Dewan-i-Arz ... Muhammad Tughlaq"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Dewan-i-bandagani ... Tughlaq</h3>
<p>The <b>Diwan-i-Bandagan</b> (department of slaves) was set up by <b>Firuz Shah Tughlaq</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Diwan-i-Mustakhraj (to recover arrears from revenue officials) was set up by <b>Alauddin Khalji</b>.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Diwan-i-Kohi (agriculture) was set up by <b>Muhammad bin Tughlaq</b>.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Diwan-i-Arz (army) was set up by <b>Balban</b>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>See 2002 Q49. Firuz also set up the Diwan-i-Khairat (charity) and Diwan-i-Istihqaq (pensions).</li>
<li>Other Sultanate offices: Diwan-i-Wizarat (finance, under the wazir), Diwan-i-Insha (correspondence), Diwan-i-Risalat (religious affairs and appeals). Alauddin's market reforms were run by the Shahna-i-Mandi.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-99", year: 2001, no: 99, subject: "ir", topic: "GATT to WTO",
  q: `The earlier name of WTO was`,
  opts: ["UNCTAD", "GATT", "UNIDO", "OECD"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) GATT</h3>
<p>The <b>General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade</b> (1947) was replaced by the <b>World Trade Organization</b> on 1 January 1995, after the Uruguay Round (Marrakesh Agreement, 1994).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> UNCTAD (1964) is a separate UN body for trade and development.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> UNIDO (Vienna) promotes industrial development.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The OECD (Paris) is a club of rich economies.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The WTO's Director-General is Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala (second term from 2025). The Appellate Body has been non-functional since 2019. MC14 was held in Yaoundé (2026).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-100", year: 2001, no: 100, subject: "science", topic: "Wire resistance", provKey: "B",
  q: `Two wires have their lengths, diameters and resistivities, all in the ratio of 1: 2. If the resistance of the thinner wire is 10 ohms, the resistance of the thicker wire is`,
  opts: ["10 ohms", "5 ohms", "20 ohms", "40 ohms"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 10 ohms</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (b). But working through R = ρL/A shows the two resistances are <b>equal</b>.</div>
<p>R = ρL/A, and A depends on d². The thicker wire has 2ρ, 2L and 2d, so its area is 4 times larger:</p>
<p>R<sub>thick</sub> = (2ρ × 2L) / (4A) = ρL/A = R<sub>thin</sub> = <b>10 Ω</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> 5 Ω ignores the doubled resistivity or length.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> 20 Ω ignores the larger area.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> 40 Ω gets the area effect backwards.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Resistance rises with length and resistivity and falls with cross-section. Copper and aluminium are used for wires because of low resistivity; nichrome is used for heaters because of high resistivity.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-101", year: 2001, no: 101, subject: "science", topic: "Bent stick in water",
  q: `Assertion (A): A stick is dipped in water in a slanting position. If observed sideways, the stick appears short and bent at the surface of water.
Reason (R): The light coming from the stick undergoes scattering from water molecules giving the stick a short and bent appearance.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT a correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) A is true but R is false</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> the submerged part looks raised, so the stick appears bent and shorter.</li>
<li><b>R (false):</b> the cause is <b>refraction</b>: light bends away from the normal as it passes from water to air. It is not scattering.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> and <b>(b)</b> need R to be true.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> A is true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Refraction also makes pools look shallower (real depth / apparent depth = refractive index, about 1.33) and stars twinkle. Scattering explains the blue sky (2002 Q69).</li>
<li>Snell's law: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂. Light bends towards the normal when entering a denser medium and away from it when leaving, which is why a fish appears higher than it really is to someone looking from the bank.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-102", year: 2001, no: 102, subject: "science", topic: "Copper feels hotter than glass", provKey: "C",
  q: `Assertion (A): A piece of copper and a piece of glass are heated to the same temperature. When touched, thereafter, the copper piece appears hotter than the glass piece.
Reason (R): The density of copper is more than that of glass.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT a correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT a correct explanation of A</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (c), treating R as false. But copper (about 8.9 g/cm³) <b>is</b> denser than glass (about 2.5 g/cm³), so R is true. It just does not explain A.</div>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> copper feels hotter because its <b>thermal conductivity</b> is far higher (about 400 W/m·K against about 1 for glass), so it passes heat to the skin much faster.</li>
<li><b>R (true, but not the reason):</b> density does not decide how hot something feels.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Density is not the explanation.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> and <b>(d)</b> Both statements are true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>For the same reason, a metal bench feels colder than a wooden one in winter, though both are at the same temperature.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-103", year: 2001, no: 103, subject: "science", topic: "Boiling point at altitude",
  q: `Assertion (A): The boiling point of water decreases as the altitude increases.
Reason (R): The atmospheric pressure increases with altitude.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT a correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) A is true but R is false</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> water boils at about 70 °C on Everest and about 90 °C at Leh.</li>
<li><b>R (false):</b> air pressure <b>decreases</b> with altitude. Lower pressure lowers the boiling point, which is the actual reason for A.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> and <b>(b)</b> need R to be true.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> A is true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pressure cookers raise the pressure to about 2 atm, which raises the boiling point to about 120 °C so food cooks faster. That is why they are essential in the hills.</li>
<li>Atmospheric pressure falls by about 1 hPa for every 8 m near the surface and is halved at about 5.5 km. Mountaineers need extra oxygen above about 8,000 m (the "death zone").</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-104", year: 2001, no: 104, subject: "science", topic: "Light entering glass",
  q: `When light waves pass from air to glass, the variables affected are`,
  opts: ["Wavelength, frequency and velocity", "Velocity and frequency", "Wavelength and frequency", "Wavelength and velocity"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Wavelength and velocity</h3>
<p>The source sets the <b>frequency</b>, and it stays the same. In glass, light <b>slows</b> (v = c/n, about 2 × 10⁸ m/s), so its <b>wavelength</b> shortens (λ = v/f).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Frequency does not change.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Frequency does not change.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Frequency does not change, and velocity does.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Colour depends on frequency, so light keeps its colour underwater. Different speeds for different colours cause dispersion (prisms, rainbows).</li>
<li>Refractive index n = c/v: glass about 1.5, water about 1.33, diamond about 2.42 (the high value plus total internal reflection gives diamonds their sparkle). Optical fibres rely on total internal reflection.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-105", year: 2001, no: 105, subject: "science", topic: "Anomalous expansion of water",
  q: `When water is heated from 0°C to 10°C, its volume`,
  opts: ["increases", "decreases", "does not change", "first decreases and then increases"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) first decreases and then increases</h3>
<p>Water <b>contracts</b> from 0 °C to <b>4 °C</b>, where it is densest (1.000 g/cm³), and then <b>expands</b> above 4 °C. This is the anomalous expansion of water, caused by hydrogen bonding.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> It does not increase all the way.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> It does not decrease all the way.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Its volume does change.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Lakes freeze from the top while water at 4 °C stays at the bottom, so fish survive winter. Ice is about 9% less dense than water, which is why icebergs float and frozen water bursts pipes.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-106", year: 2001, no: 106, subject: "science", topic: "Chain reaction in a reactor", provKey: "D",
  q: `Consider the following statements: In a nuclear reactor, self-sustained chain reaction is possible, because
<ol><li>more neutrons are released in each of the fission reactions.</li><li>the neutrons immediately take part in the fission process.</li><li>the fast neutrons are slowed down by Graphite.</li><li>every neutron released in the fission reaction initiates further fission.</li></ol>
Which of these statements are correct?`,
  opts: ["I, II and III", "I and III", "II and IV", "II, III and IV"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) I and III</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (d). But not every neutron causes another fission (many are absorbed or escape), and a reactor is controllable precisely because some neutrons are <b>delayed</b>. So statements 2 and 4 are wrong.</div>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> each U-235 fission releases 2–3 neutrons, more than the one absorbed, so the reaction can sustain itself.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> about 0.65% of neutrons are delayed by seconds, which lets control rods work.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> a moderator (graphite, heavy water or ordinary water) slows fast neutrons to thermal speeds, at which U-235 fissions easily.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> only about one neutron per fission needs to cause another fission (multiplication factor k = 1). Control rods (boron, cadmium) absorb the rest.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Indian PHWRs use heavy water as both moderator and coolant; fast breeder reactors (PFBR) use no moderator. India's nuclear capacity is about 8.8 GW, with a target of 22 GW by 2031–32 and 100 GW by 2047.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-107", year: 2001, no: 107, subject: "science", topic: "Particle properties",
  q: `Match List I with List II and select the correct answer using the codes given below the Lists :
<table><tr><th>List I (Characteristic)</th><th>List II (Particle)</th></tr>
<tr><td>I. Zero mass</td><td>A) Positron</td></tr>
<tr><td>II. Fractional charge</td><td>B) Neutrino</td></tr>
<tr><td>III. Fractional spin</td><td>C) Quark</td></tr>
<tr><td>IV. Integral spin</td><td>D) Phonon</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["I-B, II-C, III-A, IV-D", "I-C, II-B, III-D, IV-A", "I-B, II-C, III-D, IV-A", "I-C, II-B, III-A, IV-D"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) I-B, II-C, III-A, IV-D</h3>
<table><tr><th>Property</th><th>Particle</th></tr>
<tr><td>Zero mass (as then believed)</td><td>Neutrino; now known to have a tiny mass (neutrino oscillations, Nobel 2015)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fractional charge</td><td>Quark (+2/3 or −1/3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Half-integer spin</td><td>Positron (spin ½, a fermion)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Integer spin</td><td>Phonon (a quantum of sound or vibration, a boson)</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Swaps the neutrino and quark, and the positron and phonon; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Swaps the positron and phonon; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Swaps the neutrino and quark; wrong.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fermions have half-integer spin and obey Pauli's exclusion principle; bosons have integer spin (photons, phonons, the Higgs). The India-based Neutrino Observatory (Theni) has been stalled.</li>
</ul>` }
);
;
