window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2001-108", year: 2001, no: 108, subject: "science", topic: "Chandra X-ray Observatory",
  q: `Who is the scientist in whose honour the "Chandra" X­ray telescope has been named?`,
  opts: ["Chandrasekhar Venkat Raman", "Jagdish Chandra Bose", "Prafulla Chandra Roy", "Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar</h3>
<p>NASA's <b>Chandra X-ray Observatory</b> (launched 1999) is named after astrophysicist <b>S. Chandrasekhar</b> (Nobel Physics 1983), known for the Chandrasekhar limit (about 1.4 solar masses), above which a white dwarf collapses into a neutron star or black hole.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> C.V. Raman (Nobel 1930) discovered the Raman effect.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> J.C. Bose was a pioneer of radio and microwaves and of plant physiology (the crescograph).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> P.C. Ray founded Bengal Chemicals and the Indian chemical industry.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Chandra faced proposed NASA budget cuts in 2024–25. India's XPoSat (January 2024) is its first X-ray polarimetry satellite. Chandrasekhar was C.V. Raman's nephew.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-109", year: 2001, no: 109, subject: "science", topic: "Mass on the Moon",
  q: `The mass of a body on Earth is 100 kg (acceleration due to gravity, ge - 10 m/s2). If acceleration due to gravity on the Moon ge/6, then the mass of the body on the moon is`,
  opts: ["100/6 kg", "60kg", "100 kg", "600 kg"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 100 kg</h3>
<p><b>Mass</b> is the amount of matter and is the same everywhere. Only <b>weight</b> (mg) changes: 1,000 N on Earth and about 167 N on the Moon.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> 100/6 kg confuses mass with weight.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> 60 kg has no basis.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> 600 kg reverses the ratio.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Moon gravity is about 1.62 m/s² (one-sixth of Earth's). Chandrayaan-3 landed near the lunar south pole (23 August 2023, now National Space Day). Chandrayaan-4 is planned as a sample-return mission (about 2027).</li>
<li>Weight is measured with a spring balance, which shows less on the Moon; a beam balance compares masses and reads the same anywhere. Astronauts on the Moon could jump about six times higher.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-110", year: 2001, no: 110, subject: "science", topic: "Simple pendulum",
  q: `Consider the following statements: A simple pendulum is set into oscillation. Then
<ol><li>the acceleration is zero when the bob passes through the mean position.</li><li>in each cycle the bob attains a given velocity twice.</li><li>both acceleration and velocity of the bob are zero when it reaches its extreme position during its oscillation.</li><li>the amplitude of oscillation of the simple pendulum decreases with time.</li></ol>
Which of these statements are correct?`,
  opts: ["I and II", "III and IV", "I, II and IV", "II, III and IV"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) I and II (as per key)</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct, for SHM):</b> the restoring force, and so the tangential acceleration, is zero at the mean position, where speed is greatest.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> a given velocity is reached twice per cycle.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> at the extremes velocity is zero but acceleration is greatest.</li>
<li><b>4 (excluded by the key):</b> an ideal simple pendulum keeps its amplitude. A real one slowly loses amplitude to air resistance and friction (damping).</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Debated: for a real pendulum statement 4 is true, which would give (c). The key treats the pendulum as ideal and gives (a).</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Period T = 2π√(L/g): it does not depend on mass or small amplitude (Galileo). A pendulum clock runs slow in summer (longer rod) and on hills (lower g).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-111", year: 2001, no: 111, subject: "geography", topic: "Cloudy nights are warmer",
  q: `Cloudy nights are warmer compared to clear cloudless nights, because clouds`,
  opts: ["prevent cold waves from the sky from descending on Earth", "reflect back heat given off by Earth", "produce heat and radiate it towards Earth", "absorb heat from the atmosphere and send it towards Earth"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) reflect back heat given off by Earth</h3>
<p>At night the ground loses heat as infrared radiation. Clouds absorb and send much of it back down (the greenhouse effect of water), so the surface cools less. On clear nights the heat escapes to space, which brings frost and radiation fog.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> There are no "cold waves" coming down from the sky.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Clouds do not produce heat of their own.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Clouds return heat that comes from the Earth's surface, not heat gathered from the atmosphere generally.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Deserts have a large daily temperature range because of clear skies and dry air. North India's winter fog forms on clear, calm nights after rain from western disturbances.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-112", year: 2001, no: 112, subject: "geography", topic: "Falling barometer",
  q: `Which of the following weather conditions is indicated by a sudden fall in barometer reading?`,
  opts: ["Stormy weather", "Calm weather", "Cold and dry weather", "Hot and sunny Weather"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Stormy weather</h3>
<p>A <b>sudden drop</b> in pressure means a low-pressure system (a depression or cyclone) is approaching, bringing <b>storms</b>, wind and rain.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Calm weather goes with steady or rising pressure.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Cold, dry weather goes with high pressure.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Hot, sunny weather goes with high or steady pressure.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>A gradual fall means rain is likely; a gradual rise means improving weather. Mercury barometers read 76 cm at sea level (1013 hPa).</li>
<li>Isobars (lines of equal pressure) drawn close together mean strong winds. Tropical cyclones have very low central pressure (below 950 hPa in severe ones); IMD classifies them by wind speed, from depression to super cyclonic storm.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-113", year: 2001, no: 113, subject: "science", topic: "Half-life",
  q: `A radioactive substance has a half-life of four months. Three fourth of the substance would decay in`,
  opts: ["3 months", "4 months", "8 months", "12 months"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 8 months</h3>
<p>After one half-life (4 months) half remains; after two (8 months) a quarter remains, so <b>three-quarters has decayed</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> 3 months is less than one half-life (less than half decayed).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> 4 months: only half has decayed.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> 12 months: seven-eighths has decayed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Half-lives: C-14 about 5,730 years (radiocarbon dating), I-131 8 days (thyroid treatment), Co-60 5.3 years (cancer therapy and food irradiation), U-238 4.5 billion years.</li>
<li>Formula: remaining fraction = (1/2)^(t/T). After n half-lives, 1/2ⁿ remains. Radioactive decay is not affected by temperature, pressure or chemical state.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-116", year: 2001, no: 116, subject: "science", topic: "Temperature and reaction rate",
  q: `Assertion (A): A Chemical reaction becomes faster at higher temperatures.
Reason (R): At higher temperatures, molecular motion becomes more rapid.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT a correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> reaction rates roughly double for every 10 °C rise (a rule of thumb).</li>
<li><b>R (true, and the reason):</b> faster molecules collide more often and harder, so more collisions exceed the activation energy (Arrhenius).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> R does explain A.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> and <b>(d)</b> Both statements are true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Refrigeration slows food spoilage. Catalysts (and enzymes) speed reactions by lowering the activation energy, not by heating.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-117", year: 2001, no: 117, subject: "science", topic: "Radioactive elements",
  q: `Which one of the following is NOT radioactive?`,
  opts: ["Astatine", "Francium", "Tritium", "Zirconium"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Zirconium</h3>
<p><b>Zirconium</b> (Zr, 40) has stable natural isotopes. Its alloys (zircaloy) clad nuclear fuel because zirconium absorbs few neutrons.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Astatine (At, 85) is radioactive; it is the rarest natural element.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Francium (Fr, 87) is radioactive, with very short half-lives.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Tritium (H-3) is radioactive, with a half-life of about 12.3 years; it is fuel for fusion.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Every element after bismuth (83) is radioactive, as are technetium (43) and promethium (61). Japan began releasing treated Fukushima water containing tritium in 2023.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-118", year: 2001, no: 118, subject: "science", topic: "Alkane molecular weights",
  q: `Which one of the following is the correct sequence in increasing order of molecular weights of the hydrocarbons?`,
  opts: ["Methane, ethane, propane and butane", "Propane, butane, ethane and methane", "Butane, ethane, propane and methane", "Butane, propane, ethane and methane"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Methane, ethane, propane and butane</h3>
<p>Each alkane adds a CH<sub>2</sub> unit (14): methane CH<sub>4</sub> (16) < ethane C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> (30) < propane C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub> (44) < butane C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub> (58).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Wrong order.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Wrong order.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Decreasing, not increasing, order.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>LPG is propane plus butane; CNG is mostly methane (2004 Q124). Boiling points rise with chain length: the C1–C4 alkanes are gases and C5–C17 are liquids.</li>
<li>Alkanes follow the general formula C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub> and are saturated (single bonds only). Methane is a greenhouse gas about 80 times stronger than CO<sub>2</sub> over 20 years; the Global Methane Pledge aims to cut emissions 30% by 2030 (India has not joined).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-119", year: 2001, no: 119, subject: "science", topic: "Aufbau principle",
  q: `In an atom, the order of filling up of the orbitals is governed by`,
  opts: ["Aufbau principle", "Heisenberg's uncertainty principle", "Hund's rule", "Pauli's exclusion principle"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Aufbau principle</h3>
<p>The <b>Aufbau</b> ("building up") principle: electrons fill orbitals in order of rising energy (1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d …, the n + l rule).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Heisenberg: position and momentum cannot both be known exactly.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Hund's rule: in orbitals of equal energy, electrons occupy them singly before pairing.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Pauli: no two electrons share all four quantum numbers (at most 2 per orbital).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Exceptions include chromium (3d⁵4s¹) and copper (3d¹⁰4s¹), which favour half-filled or full subshells. See 2003 Q98 on quantum numbers.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-120", year: 2001, no: 120, subject: "science", topic: "Hydrolysis of copper sulphate",
  q: `An aqueous solution of copper sulphate is acidic in nature because the salt undergoes`,
  opts: ["dialysis", "electrolysis", "hydrolysis", "photolysis"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) hydrolysis</h3>
<p>CuSO<sub>4</sub> is a salt of a <b>weak base</b> (Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub>) and a <b>strong acid</b> (H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>). Cu²⁺ ions react with water (<b>hydrolysis</b>), releasing H⁺, so the solution is acidic (pH below 7).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Dialysis separates particles through a membrane (as in kidney dialysis).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Electrolysis needs an electric current.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Photolysis is splitting by light (as of water in photosynthesis).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Salt types: strong acid + strong base gives a neutral solution (NaCl); weak acid + strong base gives a basic one (Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>); strong acid + weak base gives an acidic one (NH<sub>4</sub>Cl, CuSO<sub>4</sub>). Copper sulphate is used in Bordeaux mixture (2003 Q29).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-121", year: 2001, no: 121, subject: "science", topic: "Periodic trends",
  q: `Consider the following statements with reference to the Periodic Table of chemical elements:
<ol><li>lionisation potential gradually decreases along a period.</li><li>In a group of elements, electron affinity decreases as the atomic weight increases.</li><li>In a given period, electronegativity decreases as the atomic number increases.</li></ol>
Which of these statement(s) is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["I only", "II only", "I and III", "II and III"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) II only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> ionisation energy generally <b>increases</b> across a period, because nuclear charge rises and size shrinks.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct, generally):</b> down a group, larger atoms attract added electrons less. Chlorine above fluorine is an exception.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> electronegativity <b>increases</b> across a period (fluorine is highest).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>See 2003 Q105. Noble gases have the highest ionisation energies in their periods; caesium and francium the lowest.</li>
<li>Metallic character increases down a group and decreases across a period, so the most reactive metals are at the bottom left and the most reactive non-metals at the top right (fluorine).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-122", year: 2001, no: 122, subject: "geography", topic: "Quartzite", provKey: "A",
  q: `Quartzite is metamorphosed from`,
  opts: ["Limestone", "Obsidian", "Sandstone", "Shale"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Sandstone</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (a), but limestone metamorphoses into <b>marble</b>. Quartzite forms from quartz-rich <b>sandstone</b>.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Limestone becomes marble.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Obsidian is volcanic glass, an igneous rock.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Shale becomes slate, then schist.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other pairs: granite becomes gneiss; coal becomes graphite. Makrana marble (Rajasthan) was used for the Taj Mahal.</li>
<li>Metamorphism is change by heat and pressure without melting. Regional metamorphism happens in mountain belts; contact metamorphism happens near igneous intrusions. Quartzite is very hard and is used for road metal and building.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-123", year: 2001, no: 123, subject: "science", topic: "Organelles in protein synthesis",
  q: `Which of the following cell organelles play the most significant role in protein synthesis?`,
  opts: ["Lysosome and Centrosome", "Endoplasmic reticulum and Ribosome", "Golgi apparatus and Mitochondria", "Lysosome and Mitochondria"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Endoplasmic reticulum and Ribosome</h3>
<p><b>Ribosomes</b> translate mRNA into protein. Those on the <b>rough endoplasmic reticulum</b> make proteins for export and membranes, which the ER folds and carries on.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Lysosomes digest material; centrosomes organise cell division.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Golgi packages and modifies proteins; mitochondria make ATP.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Neither lysosomes nor mitochondria are the main site of protein synthesis.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ribosomes are 70S in prokaryotes and 80S in eukaryotes, and many antibiotics target the bacterial 70S type. The smooth ER makes lipids and breaks down toxins.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-124", year: 2001, no: 124, subject: "science", topic: "Recombinant DNA", provKey: "B",
  q: `Assertion (A): Scientists can cut apart and paste together DNA molecules at will, regardless of the source of the molecules.
Reason (R): DNA fragments can be manipulated using restriction endonucleases and DNA ligases.`,
  opts: ["Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT a correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (b). But restriction enzymes (the molecular "scissors") and ligase (the "glue") are exactly what make cutting and joining DNA from any source possible.</div>
<ul>
<li><b>A (true):</b> recombinant DNA technology joins DNA from different organisms (for example the human insulin gene in bacteria).</li>
<li><b>R (true, and the reason):</b> restriction endonucleases cut at specific sequences, leaving "sticky ends", and DNA ligase seals the fragments together.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> R does explain A.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> and <b>(d)</b> Both statements are true.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cohen and Boyer made the first recombinant DNA (1973). See 2002 Q129. CRISPR-Cas9 now edits genes in place (India allowed SDN-1 and SDN-2 edits without GM rules in 2022).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-125", year: 2001, no: 125, subject: "science", topic: "Apoptosis",
  q: `The cellular and molecular control of programmed cell death is known as`,
  opts: ["Apoptosis", "Ageing", "Degeneration", "Necrosis"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Apoptosis</h3>
<p><b>Apoptosis</b> is orderly, gene-controlled cell suicide, carried out by caspase enzymes. It shapes the embryo (separating fingers) and removes damaged cells. Brenner, Horvitz and Sulston won the Nobel Prize (2002) for this work.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Ageing is gradual decline (senescence).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Degeneration is general deterioration of tissue.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Necrosis is uncontrolled death from injury, which causes inflammation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cancer cells often escape apoptosis (p53 mutations), and many chemotherapy drugs work by triggering it. Autophagy (Ohsumi, Nobel 2016) is the cell's self-recycling.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-126", year: 2001, no: 126, subject: "science", topic: "Mitochondrial DNA",
  q: `Which organelle in the cell, other than nucleus, contains DNA?`,
  opts: ["Centriole", "Golgi apparatus", "Lysosome", "Mitochondrion"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Mitochondrion</h3>
<p><b>Mitochondria</b> (and chloroplasts in plants) have their own circular DNA and 70S ribosomes, evidence of their origin as bacteria (endosymbiotic theory, Lynn Margulis).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Centrioles organise the spindle and have no DNA.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Golgi apparatus has no DNA.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Lysosomes have no DNA.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mitochondrial DNA is inherited only from the mother; it is used to trace ancestry ("mitochondrial Eve"). The UK allows mitochondrial donation ("three-parent" IVF); eight babies born this way were reported in 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-127", year: 2001, no: 127, subject: "science", topic: "Epiphytes",
  q: `Epiphytes are plants which depend on other plants for`,
  opts: ["food", "mechanical support", "shade", "water"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) mechanical support</h3>
<p><b>Epiphytes</b> (orchids, ferns, mosses, lichens) grow <b>on</b> other plants only for support and access to light. They make their own food and take water from the air (orchids' velamen roots), so they are not parasites.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Parasites (Cuscuta, Rafflesia) take food from their hosts.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> They seek light, not shade.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> They get water from rain and humidity, not from the host.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Epiphytes thrive in rainforests and the North-East's humid forests; Arunachal has over 600 orchid species. Mistletoe (Loranthus) is a partial parasite.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-128", year: 2001, no: 128, subject: "science", topic: "Antigens",
  q: `Antigen is a substance which`,
  opts: ["destroys harmful bacteria", "is used to treat poisoning", "lowers body temperature", "stimulates formation of antibody"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) stimulates formation of antibody</h3>
<p>An <b>antigen</b> is any molecule (usually a protein or polysaccharide on a microbe) that the immune system sees as foreign, triggering <b>antibodies</b> from B cells and responses from T cells.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Antibodies and phagocytes destroy bacteria; antigens do not.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Antidotes and antitoxins treat poisoning.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Antipyretics (such as paracetamol) lower fever.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Vaccines present antigens (killed or weakened microbes, subunits or mRNA) to build memory. Rapid antigen tests detect viral proteins.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-129", year: 2001, no: 129, subject: "science", topic: "DNA complementarity",
  q: `Which of the following features of DNA makes in uniquely suited to store and transmit genetic information from generation to generation?`,
  opts: ["Complementarity of the two stands", "Double helix", "Number of base pairs per turn", "Sugar phosphate backbone"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Complementarity of the two stands</h3>
<p>Because A pairs with T and G with C, each strand is a <b>template</b> for the other. That is how DNA is copied faithfully (semi-conservative replication, Meselson–Stahl 1958) and repaired.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> The double helix gives stability, but complementarity is what allows copying.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> About 10 base pairs per turn is a structural detail.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The sugar–phosphate backbone gives structure, not information.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Watson and Crick (1953) used Rosalind Franklin's X-ray data. India's Genome India project (2025) published 10,000 sequenced genomes.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-130", year: 2001, no: 130, subject: "science", topic: "Bt cotton",
  q: `The American multinational company, Monsanto has produced an insect resistant cotton variety that is undergoing field- trails in India. A toxin gene from which ONE of the following bacteria has been transferred to this transgenic cotton?`,
  opts: ["Bacillus subtilis", "Bacillus thurigiensis", "Bacillus amyloliquifanciens", "Bacillus globlii"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Bacillus thurigiensis</h3>
<p>Bt cotton carries <b>cry</b> genes (Cry1Ac, then Cry2Ab in Bollgard II) from the soil bacterium <b><i>Bacillus thuringiensis</i></b>. They make a protein that is toxic to bollworm larvae. It was approved in India in March 2002.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> <i>B. subtilis</i> is a common lab and probiotic bacterium.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> <i>B. amyloliquefaciens</i> is a source of industrial enzymes and restriction enzymes (BamHI).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> <i>B. globigii</i> was used as a harmless test agent.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bt cotton covers over 90% of India's cotton area, but pink bollworm has become resistant. Bt brinjal is under moratorium (2010). HTBt cotton is grown illegally. See 2003 Q19.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-131", year: 2001, no: 131, subject: "science", topic: "Bone names",
  q: `Match List I with List II and select the correct answer using the codes given below the Lists:
<table><tr><th>List I (Bone)</th><th>List II (Name)</th></tr>
<tr><td>I. Breast-bone</td><td>A) Clavicle</td></tr>
<tr><td>II. Collar-bone</td><td>B) Patella</td></tr>
<tr><td>III. Knee-cap</td><td>C) Scapula</td></tr>
<tr><td>IV. Shoulder blade</td><td>D) Sternum</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["I-D, II-A, III-C, IV-B", "I-A, II-D, III-C, IV-B", "I-A, II-D, III-B, IV-C", "I-D, II-A, III-B, IV-C"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I-D, II-A, III-B, IV-C</h3>
<table><tr><th>Common name</th><th>Anatomical name</th></tr>
<tr><td>Breastbone</td><td>Sternum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Collarbone</td><td>Clavicle</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kneecap</td><td>Patella</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shoulder blade</td><td>Scapula</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Swaps the patella and scapula; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Swaps the sternum and clavicle, and the patella and scapula; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Swaps the sternum and clavicle; wrong.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Adults have 206 bones. The femur is the longest, the stapes (ear) the smallest. The patella is a sesamoid bone (formed inside a tendon).</li>
<li>A newborn has about 270–300 bones, which fuse to 206. The hyoid is the only bone not joined to another bone. Osteoporosis (bone thinning) is common in older women; vitamin D and calcium are key.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-132", year: 2001, no: 132, subject: "science", topic: "Worms",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Tapeworm is a hermaphrodite.</li><li>Round-worm has separate sexes.</li><li>Filaria is caused by a nematode.</li><li>Guinea worm is an annelid.</li></ol>
Which of these are correct?`,
  opts: ["I and II", "I, II and III", "III and IV", "II, III and IV"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) I, II and III</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the tapeworm (a flatworm) is hermaphrodite; each segment has both sexes' organs.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> roundworms (<i>Ascaris</i>) have separate sexes.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> filariasis is caused by the nematode <i>Wuchereria bancrofti</i>, spread by <i>Culex</i> mosquitoes.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> the guinea worm (<i>Dracunculus</i>) is a <b>nematode</b>, not an annelid (earthworms and leeches are annelids).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India was certified free of guinea worm in 2000. It aims to eliminate lymphatic filariasis by 2027 through mass drug administration.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-133", year: 2001, no: 133, subject: "science", topic: "Genetics pioneers",
  q: `Match List I with List II and select the correct answer using the codes given below the Lists:
<table><tr><th>List I (Achievement in genetics)</th><th>List II (Scientists)</th></tr>
<tr><td>I. Discovery of tramsdiction and Conjugation in bacteria</td><td>A) Khurana</td></tr>
<tr><td>II. Establishing the sex-linked inheritance</td><td>B) Kornberg</td></tr>
<tr><td>III. Isolation of DNA polymerase form E. coli</td><td>C) Lederberg</td></tr>
<tr><td>IV. Establishing the complete genetic code</td><td>D) Morgan</td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td>E) Ochoa</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["I-D, II-C, III-B, IV-A", "I-C, II-D, III-A, IV-E", "I-D, II-C, III-A, IV-E", "I-C, II-D, III-B, IV-A"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I-C, II-D, III-B, IV-A</h3>
<table><tr><th>Achievement</th><th>Scientist</th></tr>
<tr><td>Transduction and conjugation in bacteria</td><td>Joshua Lederberg (Nobel 1958)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sex-linked inheritance (fruit flies)</td><td>Thomas Hunt Morgan (Nobel 1933)</td></tr>
<tr><td>DNA polymerase I from <i>E. coli</i></td><td>Arthur Kornberg (Nobel 1959, with Ochoa)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Completing the genetic code</td><td>Har Gobind Khorana (Nobel 1968, with Nirenberg and Holley)</td></tr></table>
<div class="note">"tramsdiction" in the source is a typo for transduction.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Swaps Lederberg and Morgan; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Gives DNA polymerase to Khorana and the code to Ochoa; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Wrong on all four.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ochoa found an RNA-making enzyme (polynucleotide phosphorylase). Khorana also made the first synthetic gene (1972).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-134", year: 2001, no: 134, subject: "science", topic: "Athlete's foot",
  q: `"Athlete's Foot" is a disease caused by`,
  opts: ["Bacteria", "Fungus", "Protozoan", "Nematode"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Fungus</h3>
<p>Athlete's foot (<i>tinea pedis</i>) is a <b>fungal</b> infection by dermatophytes (<i>Trichophyton</i>), favoured by warm, damp feet. See 2004 Q141.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Bacteria cause diseases such as cholera, TB and typhoid.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Protozoa cause malaria and amoebiasis.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Nematodes cause filariasis and ascariasis.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other tinea infections: ringworm (corporis), jock itch (cruris). India has seen a surge of hard-to-treat, drug-resistant dermatophyte infections (<i>T. indotineae</i>) since about 2017.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-135", year: 2001, no: 135, subject: "science", topic: "Eye donation",
  q: `In the eye donation, which part of the eye is transplanted from the donor?`,
  opts: ["Cornea", "Lens", "Retina", "The whole eye"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Cornea</h3>
<p>Donated eyes provide the <b>cornea</b> (the clear front window), which is grafted to treat corneal blindness (keratoplasty). The cornea has no blood vessels, so rejection is uncommon.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> A cloudy lens (cataract) is replaced with an artificial intraocular lens, not a donor lens.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The retina cannot be transplanted.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Whole-eye transplants are not possible (the optic nerve cannot be reconnected).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Eyes must be removed within 6–8 hours of death. India's National Eye Donation Fortnight is 25 August–8 September.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-136", year: 2001, no: 136, subject: "science", topic: "Universal donor", provKey: "B",
  q: `A man whose blood group is not known meets with a serious accident and needs blood transfusion immediately. Which one of the blood groups mentioned below and readily available in the hospital will be safe for transfusion?`,
  opts: ["O, Rh-", "O, Rh+", "AB, Rh-", "AB, Rh+"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) O, Rh-</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (b). But <b>O Rh-negative</b> is the universal donor. Its red cells carry no A, B or D (Rh) antigens, so it is safe for anyone, including Rh-negative patients. O Rh-positive could sensitise an Rh-negative recipient.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> O Rh+ is widely used in emergencies for men, but it is not safe for every patient whose group is unknown.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> AB Rh- is the universal plasma donor, not the red-cell donor.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> AB Rh+ is the universal <b>recipient</b>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>About 94% of Indians are Rh-positive. The rare "Bombay blood group" (hh) was found in Mumbai (1952). A new blood group, "CRIB", was reported from Karnataka in 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-137", year: 2001, no: 137, subject: "science", topic: "Metastasis", provKey: "A",
  q: `"Metastasis" is the process by which`,
  opts: ["cells divide rapidly under the influence of drugs", "cancer cells spread through the blood or laymphatic system to other sites or organs", "the chromosomes in cell nuclei are attached to the spindle before moving to the anaphase poles", "cancer cells are successfully inhibited to divide any further"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) cancer cells spread through the blood or laymphatic system to other sites or organs</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (a), which is plainly wrong. <b>Metastasis</b> is the spread of cancer from its first site to distant organs through the blood or lymph.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Drug-induced rapid division is not metastasis.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Chromosomes attach to the spindle in metaphase of mitosis.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Stopping cancer cells from dividing is the aim of chemotherapy.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Benign tumours stay put; malignant tumours invade and spread. India reports about 15 lakh new cancer cases a year. Customs duty on key cancer drugs was cut (Budgets 2024–25), and day-care cancer centres are being set up in all district hospitals.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2001-138", year: 2001, no: 138, subject: "science", topic: "Physiological roles",
  q: `Match List I with List II and select the correct answer using the codes given below the Lists :
<table><tr><th>List I (Substance)</th><th>List II (Physiological role)</th></tr>
<tr><td>I. Ptyalin</td><td>A) Converts angioten-sinogen in blood into angiotensin</td></tr>
<tr><td>II. Pepsin</td><td>B) Digests starch</td></tr>
<tr><td>III. Renin</td><td>C) Digests proteins</td></tr>
<tr><td>IV. Oxytocin</td><td>D) Hydrolyses fats</td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td>E) Induces contraction of smooth muscles</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["I-B, II-C, III-A, IV-E", "I-C, II-D, III-B, IV-E", "I-B, II-C, IH-E, IV-A", "I-C, II-A, III-B, IV-D"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) I-B, II-C, III-A, IV-E</h3>
<table><tr><th>Substance</th><th>Role</th></tr>
<tr><td>Ptyalin (salivary amylase)</td><td>Starts digesting starch in the mouth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pepsin (stomach)</td><td>Digests proteins</td></tr>
<tr><td>Renin (kidney)</td><td>Converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, which raises blood pressure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Oxytocin (posterior pituitary)</td><td>Contracts smooth muscle of the uterus (labour) and breast (milk release)</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Makes ptyalin digest proteins and pepsin digest fats; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Swaps renin and oxytocin; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong on all four.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Do not confuse renin with <b>rennin</b> (chymosin, which curdles milk in calves). Lipase hydrolyses fats. ACE inhibitors, common blood-pressure drugs, block the renin–angiotensin system.</li>
</ul>` }
);
;
