// ---------------- Ancient History ----------------
addNote("ancient", "Harappan civilisation", `
<h3>Reading archaeological evidence (inference questions)</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Find</th><th>Valid inference</th><th>Invalid inference</th></tr>
<tr><td>Spindle-whorls in houses, no spinning wheel</td><td>Hand spinning was laborious household work</td><td>Mechanised textile industry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Graduated scales (Lothal ivory, Mohenjo-daro shell), standard chert weights (binary then decimal)</td><td>Mathematical and scientific knowledge, regulated trade</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Large baked-brick houses with private courtyards, wells, baths beside one-room tenements</td><td>Private property, social stratification</td><td>Common property system</td></tr>
</table></div>`);

addNote("ancient", "Vedic age: texts, economy and geography", `
<h3>The four Vedas</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Veda</th><th>Content</th><th>Priest</th></tr>
<tr><td>Rigveda</td><td>10 mandalas, 1,028 hymns. Purusha Sukta (X.90, four varnas), Nasadiya Sukta (creation), Nadi Sukta (X.75, rivers). Kshetrapati (lord of the field) in RV 4.57</td><td>Hotri</td></tr>
<tr><td>Samaveda</td><td>Melodies, the root of Indian music</td><td>Udgatri</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yajurveda</td><td>Sacrificial formulas. Shukla and Krishna</td><td>Adhvaryu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Atharvaveda</td><td>Charms, healing, agriculture. <b>Kshetra-patni</b> (mistress of the field, AV 2.12.1). Sabha and samiti "daughters of Prajapati"</td><td>Brahman</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Each Veda: Samhita → Brahmanas → Aranyakas → Upanishads. Six Vedangas: Shiksha, Kalpa (incl. Shulba sutras), Vyakarana (Panini's <i>Ashtadhyayi</i>), Nirukta (Yaska), Chhanda (Pingala), Jyotisha (Lagadha).</p>
<h3>Rigvedic economy</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pastoral emphasis: cattle as wealth. Gavishti (war = search for cows), gopati (chief).</li>
<li>Agriculture known: plough (langala, sira), furrow (sita), barley (yava). Well irrigation: <b>avata</b> (well), <b>ashma-chakra</b> (stone pulley), <b>ahava</b> (strapped pails), varatra (strap). Draught oxen (used even in Harappan times) likely turned the pulleys (Irfan Habib).</li>
<li>Ayas = copper/bronze. Iron (krishna ayas) and rice only in the Later Vedic period. Nishka = gold ornament or unit of value.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Rivers: Vedic names</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Vedic</th><th>Modern</th><th>Greek</th></tr>
<tr><td>Vitasta</td><td>Jhelum</td><td>Hydaspes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Asikni</td><td>Chenab</td><td>Akesines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parushni (Iravati)</td><td>Ravi</td><td>Hydraotes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vipas</td><td>Beas</td><td>Hyphasis</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shutudri</td><td>Sutlej</td><td>Zaradros</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kubha, Krumu, Gomati, Suvastu</td><td>Kabul, Kurram, Gomal, Swat</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Yavyavati</td><td>Zhob (usual identification)</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Sadanira</td><td>Gandak</td><td></td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Memory aid going east: <b>V-A-P-V-S</b> (Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas, Sutlej). The Battle of Ten Kings was fought on the Parushni.</p>
<h3>Pleistocene river links</h3>
<p>The same <i>Platanista</i> dolphins live in the Indus and Ganga systems, which implies that the Yamuna once flowed into the Indus or the Sutlej into the Yamuna (Irfan Habib). The Sutlej's westward and the Yamuna's eastward shift is also linked to the drying of the Ghaggar-Hakra (identified with the Sarasvati). Robert Bruce Foote, the "father of Indian prehistory", found the first palaeolithic tool at Pallavaram (1863), in the south, not on the Sutlej.</p>`);

addNote("ancient", "Second urbanisation, coins and religions", `
<h3>Coins and money economy</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pali texts give the first definite references to coins: <b>kahapana</b> (karshapana), nikkha, kamsa, pada, masaka, <b>kakanika</b>. Archaeology confirms it with <b>punch-marked coins</b>, mostly silver. Both point to the rise of towns and a money economy (6th century BCE onward).</li>
<li>Later: Indo-Greek portrait coins, Kushana gold, Satavahana lead coins, Gupta gold <b>dinaras</b>, Roman coins in the south.</li>
<li>Signs of the second urbanisation: iron, wet rice, NBPW pottery, fortified towns, guilds (shreni), Brahmi script.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Jainism</h3>
<ul>
<li>Four <b>gatis</b> (states of existence): Deva, Manushya, Tiryancha (animals and plants), Naraki (hell). Yakshas are attendant deities, not a separate gati.</li>
<li>Triratna. Five vows (Mahavira added brahmacharya). Anekantavada and syadvada. Jiva in all things, so strict ahimsa.</li>
<li>Councils: Pataliputra (about 300 BCE), Vallabhi (5th century CE, canon written in Ardhamagadhi). Shvetambara and Digambara.</li>
</ul>`);

addNote("ancient", "Science and mathematics", `
<ul>
<li><b>Place-value system:</b> earliest epigraphic use in the <b>Mankani plates</b> (Gujarat, 595–96 CE). General in Indian inscriptions by the 9th century. In Southeast Asian Sanskrit inscriptions by the late 7th century (Sambor, Cambodia, 683 CE, has the oldest surviving zero dot).</li>
<li>Bakhshali manuscript (dot zero). Gwalior Chaturbhuja temple inscription (876 CE, circle zero).</li>
<li>Aryabhata (499 CE): place value, π ≈ 3.1416, Earth's rotation. Brahmagupta (628 CE): rules for zero and negatives. Bhaskara II: <i>Lilavati</i>.</li>
<li>Transmission: al-Khwarizmi (9th century) → Fibonacci (1202): "Hindu-Arabic numerals".</li>
</ul>`);

addNote("ancient", "South India: Pallavas, Sangam and rivals", `
<h3>Sangam kingdoms</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Dynasty</th><th>Emblem</th><th>Capital / port</th><th>Kings</th></tr>
<tr><td>Chera</td><td>Bow</td><td>Vanji / Muziris</td><td><b>Udiyanjeral</b> (earliest), Nedunjeral Athan, <b>Senguttuvan</b> (Pattini/Kannagi cult)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chola</td><td>Tiger</td><td>Uraiyur / Puhar</td><td><b>Karikala</b> (battle of Venni, Kallanai)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pandya</td><td>Fish</td><td>Madurai / Korkai</td><td><b>Nedunjeliyan</b> (battle of Talaiyalanganam)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Sources: Tolkappiyam, Ettuthogai, Pattuppattu, <i>Silappadikaram</i>, <i>Manimekalai</i>, Ashoka's RE II, Kharavela's Hathigumpha inscription, the <i>Periplus</i>.</p>`);

