// data/book_polity_p1.js
// Polity book, Unit 1: Political theory and the idea of a constitution.
addBookTopic("polity", "p1-1", `
<p>Before studying India's Constitution, it helps to be clear about the basic ideas it rests on: what a State is, why democracy is valued, and how rights, liberty, equality and law relate to one another. UPSC regularly tests these concepts, using the language of political theory (NCERT Class XI, <i>Political Theory</i>).</p>

<h3>The State</h3>
<p>A State has four essential elements: <b>population, territory, government and sovereignty</b>. The classic definition (J.W. Garner) is a community of persons <b>permanently occupying a definite territory</b>, <b>independent of external control</b> (sovereignty), and possessing an <b>organised government</b>.</p>
<ul>
<li>A definition that lists only functions (law and order, protecting rights) misses sovereignty.</li>
<li>Shared culture, tradition and history describe a <b>nation</b>, not a State.</li>
<li>Particular institutions, such as a responsible executive or an independent judiciary, are not essential to every State.</li>
<li>The <b>Montevideo Convention</b> (1933) requires a permanent population, a defined territory, a government and the capacity to enter into relations with other States.</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Nation</td><td>A people bound by a shared identity (culture, language, history)</td></tr>
<tr><td>State</td><td>A legal and political unit with sovereignty</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nation-state</td><td>Where the two coincide</td></tr>
<tr><td>Government</td><td>The agency through which the State acts. Governments change; the State continues.</td></tr></table>
<p>In the Constitution, "State" for the purpose of Fundamental Rights has a wider meaning (Article 12; see 4.1).</p>

<h3>Democracy and its virtue</h3>
<p>Democracy's superior virtue is that it <b>calls into activity the intelligence and character of ordinary men and women</b>. Citizens develop judgement and moral character by taking part in public decisions; J.S. Mill called this the educative argument. Democracy rests on faith in the common person.</p>
<ul>
<li>Strong executive leadership, a dynamic superior individual or a band of dedicated party workers can all exist in non-democratic systems too, so none of them is democracy's distinctive virtue.</li>
<li><b>Other arguments for democracy:</b> accountable and responsive government, peaceful change of rulers, equality and dignity of citizens, protection of rights, better decisions through debate, and the ability to correct mistakes.</li>
<li><b>Criticisms:</b> slow decisions, tyranny of the majority, populism, and money and muscle power in elections.</li>
<li><b>Types:</b> direct (Swiss cantons, referendums), representative, deliberative and participatory democracy.</li>
<li>Abraham Lincoln called it government "of the people, by the people, for the people".</li>
</ul>

<h3>Rights</h3>
<p><b>Rights are claims of citizens against the State</b>: justified claims of individuals, recognised by society and enforced by the State, which limit the State's power over the individual.</p>
<ul>
<li>They are not the State's claims against citizens, and not privileges for a few; they belong equally to all.</li>
<li>Laski: rights are "those conditions of social life without which no man can seek, in general, to be himself at his best."</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Type of right</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Natural rights</td><td>Locke's life, liberty and property</td></tr>
<tr><td>Moral rights</td><td>Backed by conscience, not law</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legal rights</td><td>Created by statute</td></tr>
<tr><td>Human rights</td><td>Universal (UDHR)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fundamental rights</td><td>Constitutional and enforceable in court</td></tr></table>
<p>Most of India's Fundamental Rights run against the State. A few also bind private persons: Articles 15(2), 17, 23 and 24.</p>
<h4>Rights and duties</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Rights and duties are correlative.</b> One person's right is everyone else's duty to respect it, and a citizen who claims rights must also perform duties.
  <ul><li>Laski: "My rights are built upon my duties."</li>
  <li>Gandhi: "The true source of rights is duty."</li></ul></li>
<li>Rights are social, not "personal and independent of society". Both rights and duties matter for developing the personality and for the State's stability.</li>
<li>In Indian law, the Fundamental Duties (Article 51A) are not enforceable counterparts of the Fundamental Rights (see 4.5). The correlation is a principle of political theory.</li>
</ul>
<h4>The right to vote</h4>
<ul>
<li>UPSC's key calls the right to vote and to be elected a <b>constitutional right</b>. Article 326 provides adult suffrage for citizens aged 18 and over who are not disqualified, and Articles 84 and 173 set the qualifications for election.</li>
<li>It is <b>not a Fundamental Right</b>, since it is not in Part III. Nor is it a natural right.</li>
<li>Courts have also called it a <b>statutory right</b> under the Representation of the People Act, 1951 (N.P. Ponnuswami 1952, Jyoti Basu 1982, Kuldip Nayar 2006). <b>Freedom of voting</b>, the expression of choice including NOTA, falls under Article 19(1)(a) (PUCL 2003, 2013).</li>
<li>The voting age was lowered from 21 to 18 by the <b>61st Amendment (1988)</b>. Election law is covered in 12.1.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Liberty</h3>
<table><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Thinkers</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Negative liberty</b></td><td>Absence of restraint: "freedom from" interference</td><td>Hobbes, Bentham; Isaiah Berlin's "Two Concepts of Liberty" (1958)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Positive liberty</b></td><td>The <b>opportunity to develop oneself fully</b>: "freedom to"</td><td>T.H. Green, Harold Laski</td></tr></table>
<p>Laski described liberty as "the eager maintenance of that atmosphere in which men have the opportunity to be their best selves".</p>
<ul>
<li>"Opportunity to do whatever one likes" is <b>licence</b>, not liberty.</li>
<li>"Protection against the tyranny of rulers" is part of liberty but too narrow a definition.</li>
<li>J.S. Mill's <i>On Liberty</i> (1859) gave the <b>harm principle</b>: liberty may be restricted only to prevent harm to others.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key"><b>Law and liberty</b>"<b>If there are no laws, there is no liberty.</b>" John Locke: "Where there is no law, there is no freedom." Without law the strong would dominate the weak (Hobbes's "state of nature"). So law creates the conditions for liberty, and the two are <b>complementary</b>.<ul>
<li>"More laws means less liberty" reflects the narrow negative view.</li>
<li>"Laws made by the people" describes democracy.</li>
<li>"Frequent changes endanger liberty" may be true, but it is not the essential relationship.</li></ul></div>
<p>In the Constitution, the Preamble promises liberty of thought, expression, belief, faith and worship. Articles 19–22 and 25–28 guarantee freedoms, subject to "reasonable restrictions" that keep liberty compatible with order.</p>
<h4>Safeguards of liberty</h4>
<p>The best safeguard of liberty in a liberal democracy is the <b>separation of powers</b>. Montesquieu (<i>The Spirit of the Laws</i>, 1748) argued that liberty is safe only when legislative, executive and judicial powers are held by separate bodies that check one another.</p>
<ul>
<li>A "<b>committed judiciary</b>" (committed to the government's ideology, a phrase from the 1970s) is the opposite of an independent one.</li>
<li><b>Centralisation</b> threatens liberty.</li>
<li>An <b>elected government</b> without checks can become a tyranny of the majority.</li>
<li><b>In India</b> separation is functional rather than rigid, because the executive is drawn from the legislature. There are checks and balances: judicial review, impeachment, no-confidence motions, questions, and ordinances that need legislative approval.
  <ul><li>Article 50 (a Directive Principle) asks the State to separate the judiciary from the executive.</li>
  <li>Separation of powers is part of the basic structure (Indira Gandhi v. Raj Narain, 1975).</li></ul></li>
</ul>

<h3>Equality</h3>
<p>Equality implies the <b>absence of privileges</b>. Laski described it as, first, "the absence of special privileges" and, second, "adequate opportunities laid open to all".</p>
<ul>
<li>It does <b>not</b> mean the absence of restraints (laws apply to all), of competition (which can be fair), or of ideology.</li>
<li><b>In the Constitution:</b> the Preamble promises equality of status and of opportunity. Articles 14–18 put this into effect: Article 18 abolishes titles and Article 17 untouchability, removing privileges directly.</li>
<li><b>Formal equality</b> (equal treatment in law) is complemented by <b>substantive equality</b> (affirmative action under Articles 15(4) and 16(4)).</li>
<li>Kinds of equality: political, social and economic; equality of opportunity versus equality of outcome.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ideologies: Gandhism and Marxism</h3>
<p>Gandhism and Marxism share one goal: the <b>final goal of a stateless society</b>.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Marx:</b> after the dictatorship of the proletariat, the State "withers away" into communism.</li>
<li><b>Gandhi:</b> philosophical anarchism. His ideal was "enlightened anarchy", or Ram Rajya, of self-governing village republics where each person rules himself.</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Gandhism</th><th>Marxism</th></tr>
<tr><td>Class</td><td>Class harmony, change of heart</td><td>Class struggle</td></tr>
<tr><td>Property</td><td>Trusteeship: the rich hold wealth in trust for society</td><td>Abolition of private property</td></tr>
<tr><td>Basis of society</td><td>Moral and spiritual forces</td><td>Economic determinism (historical materialism)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Means</td><td>Pure ends need pure means; non-violence</td><td>Violent revolution acceptable</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>Gandhian ideas: satyagraha, ahimsa, <i>sarvodaya</i> (from Ruskin's <i>Unto This Last</i>), swadeshi, decentralisation, bread labour, the constructive programme.</li>
<li>The Gandhian Directive Principles are Articles 40, 43, 46, 47 and 48 (see 4.4).</li>
</ul>

<h3>The right to the city</h3>
<p>The "Right to the City" is a concept put forward by the French philosopher <b>Henri Lefebvre</b> (<i>Le Droit à la ville</i>, 1968).</p>
<ul>
<li>It gives every occupant of the city the right to <b>use, shape and take part in decisions</b> about the city, including <b>reclaiming public spaces</b>.</li>
<li>It implies that residents of <b>unauthorised colonies</b> should not be denied basic public services. UPSC's key accepted this, though no Indian law states it so absolutely.</li>
<li>It is <b>not</b> an agreed, binding human right, and UN-Habitat does <b>not</b> monitor country commitments under it. It is referred to in the New Urban Agenda (Habitat III, Quito, 2016).</li>
<li>Related: UN-Habitat (Nairobi), SDG 11, World Cities Day (31 October), PM-UDAY (2019) for Delhi's unauthorised colonies, and urban local government (10.2).</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p1-2", `
<p>Having a constitution is not the same as having constitutionalism. Constitutionalism means government is limited by a higher law. Its companions are the rule of law and the requirement that laws be fair, not merely properly passed.</p>

<h3>What a constitution is for</h3>
<p>The <b>chief purpose</b> of a constitution is to <b>define and limit the powers of government</b>. A constitution sets up the organs of the State and allocates their powers, but above all it restrains them, to protect citizens' rights and prevent arbitrary rule.</p>
<p>NCERT lists its functions:</p>
<ul>
<li>It provides basic rules that allow <b>coordination</b> and assurance among members of society.</li>
<li>It specifies <b>who has the power</b> to make decisions.</li>
<li>It sets <b>limits on government</b>: fundamental rights and the separation of powers.</li>
<li>It <b>enables</b> the government to meet society's aspirations and create a just society (the Directive Principles).</li>
<li>It expresses a people's <b>fundamental identity</b>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Creating offices, setting the objectives of laws, and securing social justice are all functions, but limiting power is the defining one.</p>

<h3>Constitutional government</h3>
<div class="key"><b>Constitutional government = limited government</b>A constitutional government is one limited by the terms of the constitution. It places effective restrictions on the <b>authority of the State in the interest of individual liberty</b>, not on liberty in the interest of State authority.<br><br>It can be federal or unitary, parliamentary or presidential. Its head may hold nominal power (parliamentary) or real power (presidential). Its legislature may be popular and multi-party. None of these is the definition.</div>
<ul>
<li><b>Elements:</b> rule of law, separation of powers, judicial review, fundamental rights, free elections, an independent judiciary and accountability.</li>
<li>A country can have a written constitution without constitutionalism; many dictatorships do.</li>
<li><b>Britain</b> has constitutional government without a written constitution: it is limited by convention, the rule of law and the Human Rights Act 1998.</li>
<li><b>In India</b> the courts enforce the limits through judicial review and the basic structure doctrine (see 7.2).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Rule of law</h3>
<p>A.V. Dicey (1885) set out three features of the rule of law:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Supremacy of law</b>: no arbitrary power, so the powers of rulers are <b>limited</b>.</li>
<li><b>Equality before law</b>: everyone, officials included, is subject to the ordinary courts.</li>
<li><b>Liberty and civil rights</b> of individuals are secured by the courts.</li>
</ol>
<p>"People's responsibility to the government" is <b>not</b> a feature; it is the government that is responsible to the people.</p>
<ul>
<li>India's <b>Article 14</b> combines "equality before law" (the British idea) with "equal protection of laws" (from the US Constitution).</li>
<li>The rule of law is part of the <b>basic structure</b> (Indira Gandhi v. Raj Narain, 1975).</li>
<li><b>Exceptions:</b> immunities of the President and Governors (Article 361), foreign diplomats, and legislators' privileges.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Arbitrariness and Article 14</h3>
<p>A law that gives the executive <b>unguided and uncontrolled discretion</b> in applying it violates <b>Article 14</b>, because officials could treat similar people differently.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>State of West Bengal v. Anwar Ali Sarkar</b> (1952) struck down a law that let the State pick, without guidelines, which cases went to special courts.</li>
<li><b>E.P. Royappa</b> (1974): "equality is antithetic to arbitrariness".</li>
<li>Maneka Gandhi (1978) and Ajay Hasia (1981) extended the principle.</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Test under Article 14</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Reasonable classification</td><td>The law must rest on an intelligible differentia with a rational nexus to its object</td></tr>
<tr><td>Manifest arbitrariness</td><td>Used to strike down laws in Shayara Bano (triple talaq, 2017), Navtej Johar (2018) and Joseph Shine (adultery, 2018)</td></tr></table>
<p>The other articles in that question:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Article 28:</b> no religious instruction in wholly State-funded institutions.</li>
<li><b>Article 32:</b> the right to constitutional remedies.</li>
<li><b>Article 44:</b> the Uniform Civil Code, a Directive Principle. Uttarakhand's UCC came into force in January 2025; Gujarat (March 2026) and Assam (May 2026) passed their own, all exempting Scheduled Tribes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Due process of law</h3>
<p>In essence, due process means the <b>principle of natural justice</b>. A law that takes away life, liberty or property must follow the prescribed procedure, and the law itself must be <b>fair, just and reasonable</b>. It comes from the 5th and 14th Amendments of the US Constitution.</p>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Procedure established by law</th><th>Due process of law</th></tr>
<tr><td>Origin</td><td>Japanese Constitution (British tradition)</td><td>US Constitution</td></tr>
<tr><td>Test</td><td>Was the law validly made and the procedure followed?</td><td>Is the law itself fair, just and not arbitrary?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protects against</td><td>The executive only</td><td>Both the legislature and the executive</td></tr></table>
<ul>
<li>Article 21 uses "<b>procedure established by law</b>". The Constituent Assembly dropped "due process" on B.N. Rau's advice, after he met US Justice Frankfurter.</li>
<li><b>A.K. Gopalan</b> (1950) read Article 21 narrowly. <b>Maneka Gandhi</b> (1978) held that the procedure must be "right, just and fair, not arbitrary, fanciful or oppressive". This effectively brought substantive due process into Article 21, read together with Articles 14 and 19, the "golden triangle".</li>
<li><b>Principles of natural justice:</b>
  <ul><li><i>nemo judex in causa sua</i>: no one should be a judge in their own cause (the rule against bias);</li>
  <li><i>audi alteram partem</i>: hear the other side;</li>
  <li>decisions should be reasoned.</li></ul></li>
<li>"Fair application of law" is part of due process, and "equality before law" is Article 14. Neither is its essence.</li>
</ul>
`);

addBookTopic("polity", "p1-3", `
<p>Constitutions organise government in two dimensions:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>horizontally</b>, between the executive and the legislature: parliamentary or presidential;</li>
<li><b>vertically</b>, between the Centre and the regions: federal or unitary.</li>
</ul>
<p>India combines a parliamentary executive with a federation that has a strong Centre.</p>

<h3>Parliamentary government</h3>
<div class="key"><b>The defining feature</b>A parliamentary system is one in which <b>the government is responsible to the Parliament and can be removed by it</b>. In India, the <b>Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha</b> (Article 75(3)) and stays in office only while it has the House's confidence.</div>
<p>Distractors that do <b>not</b> define it:</p>
<ul>
<li>A directly elected Lok Sabha: the US House is also directly elected.</li>
<li>Parliament's power to amend the Constitution.</li>
<li>A permanent Rajya Sabha: the US Senate is also continuous.</li>
<li>All parties sharing government: that is a national government.</li>
<li>A government elected by and removable by the people: that is direct election.</li>
<li>A fixed term: that is a presidential feature.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Features of the parliamentary system</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Nominal and real executives:</b> the President is head of State; the PM and Council of Ministers hold real power.</li>
<li><b>Majority party rule</b> and political homogeneity.</li>
<li><b>Collective responsibility</b> to the lower house.</li>
<li><b>Double membership:</b> <b>ministers are members of Parliament</b>. A non-member must become one within six months (Article 75(5)).</li>
<li>Ministers hold office <b>while they enjoy the confidence</b> of the House.</li>
<li><b>Leadership of the Prime Minister</b>, who heads the Cabinet. The Head of State does <b>not</b> head the Cabinet.</li>
<li><b>Dissolution</b> of the lower house is possible.</li>
</ul>
<p>Articles 74–75 set up the system at the Centre and Articles 163–164 in the States. The Swaran Singh Committee (1976) favoured keeping it.</p>

<h4>Merits and demerits</h4>
<ul>
<li>The <b>main advantage</b> is that the <b>executive remains responsible to the legislature</b>. It can be questioned and removed at any time through question hour, zero hour, adjournment, no-confidence and cut motions, and committees (see 6.3 and 6.5).</li>
<li>An executive and legislature working <b>independently</b>, and stability or <b>continuity of policy</b>, are claimed merits of the <b>presidential</b> system.</li>
<li>In a parliamentary system the head of government <b>can be changed without an election</b>, through a change of party leader or a no-confidence vote.</li>
<li>Other merits: harmony between executive and legislature, prevention of despotism, wide representation, and an alternative government always ready.</li>
<li>Demerits: instability (coalitions), "cabinet dictatorship" when majorities are large, and ministers who may lack expertise.</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Parliamentary</th><th>Presidential (USA)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Nominal and real executive</td><td>A single executive; the President is head of both State and government</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ministers are legislators</td><td>Secretaries cannot sit in Congress</td></tr>
<tr><td>Collective responsibility; removable</td><td>Fixed term; removable only by impeachment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lower house can be dissolved</td><td>No dissolution</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fusion of powers</td><td>Separation of powers</td></tr></table>

<h4>India and Britain compared</h4>
<p>India adopted the British (Westminster) model with important differences:</p>
<ul>
<li>The British Parliament is <b>sovereign</b>: no court can strike down its Acts (Dicey). India's Parliament is <b>limited</b> by a written Constitution, the federal division of powers, Fundamental Rights, judicial review and the basic structure (Kesavananda Bharati, 1973).</li>
<li>Questions on the constitutionality of laws and amendments go to a <b>Constitution Bench</b> of at least five Supreme Court judges (Article 145(3)).</li>
</ul>
<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Britain</th><th>India</th></tr>
<tr><td>Head of State</td><td>Hereditary monarch</td><td>Elected President (a republic)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Constitution</td><td>Unwritten</td><td>Written; the longest</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prime Minister</td><td>Must be from the Commons (convention)</td><td>Can be from either House</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ministers</td><td>Must be MPs</td><td>A non-member can serve for six months</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legal responsibility of ministers</td><td>Yes (countersignature)</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shadow cabinet</td><td>A formal institution</td><td>None</td></tr></table>
<p>India thus blends parliamentary sovereignty with judicial supremacy, a middle path between the British and American systems.</p>

<h3>Federalism</h3>
<p>Federalism means power is divided between the Centre and the States by a <b>written, rigid constitution</b>, with an <b>independent judiciary</b> as umpire.</p>
<div class="key"><b>The essential federal feature</b>UPSC's key names the <b>safeguarded independence of the judiciary</b>. The courts guard the division of powers and settle Centre–State disputes; the Supreme Court has original jurisdiction under Article 131.<ul>
<li>Elected representatives from the units, or regional parties in the Cabinet, also occur in unitary or coalition settings, though bicameralism is itself a classic federal trait.</li>
<li>Enforceable Fundamental Rights are a feature of constitutionalism, not of federalism.</li></ul></div>
<table><tr><th>Federal features</th><th>Unitary (non-federal) features</th></tr>
<tr><td>Dual polity; written, rigid constitution; division of powers (Seventh Schedule); supremacy of the Constitution; independent judiciary; bicameralism</td><td>Strong Centre; single constitution and single citizenship; flexible amendment; integrated judiciary; all-India services; emergency provisions; appointed Governor; Parliament can alter States (Article 3); unequal representation in the Rajya Sabha</td></tr></table>
<h4>What Indian federalism is not</h4>
<ul>
<li>It is <b>not the result of an agreement among the federating units</b>. India is a "holding together" federation, created from a unitary British India. The USA is a "coming together" federation.</li>
<li>Ambedkar called India a "<b>Union of States</b>" because the Union is indestructible, the States did not agree to form it, and they have no right to secede.</li>
<li>Its real features include an independent judiciary, a clear division of powers, and <b>unequal representation</b> of States in the Rajya Sabha by population (UP 31 seats; small States 1). In the US Senate every state has two seats.</li>
</ul>
<h4>How scholars describe it</h4>
<ul>
<li>"<b>Quasi-federal</b>": K.C. Wheare.</li>
<li>"<b>Cooperative federalism</b>": Granville Austin.</li>
<li>"A federation with a <b>strong centralising tendency</b>": Ivor Jennings.</li>
<li>"<b>Bargaining federalism</b>": Morris-Jones.</li>
<li>"<b>Competitive federalism</b>": NITI Aayog.</li>
</ul>
<p>Federalism is part of the <b>basic structure</b> (S.R. Bommai, 1994). Centre–State relations are covered in Unit 9.</p>
`);
;
